L1.1-0 - in progress
-------------
The L is for Linux, and parallels the W of SD Core for Windows.  The number is
1.1 to match SD Core for Windows W1.1-0, the release this one is built to conform
with: SD Core for Linux tracks SD Core for Windows, and sharing the version
number makes it plain which pair of builds a screenshot or a support question
belongs to.

There was no shipped L1.0 - this is the FIRST release of SD Core for Linux,
numbered to line up with Windows rather than incremented from a Linux
predecessor.  1.0-1 and 1.0-2 were releases of the SD this work was built from,
not releases of this system.  So everything below is what L1.1-0 contains, not
what changed since some earlier Linux release.

Entries are newest first, by date.  This section is written for someone using
SD: what changed, why it mattered, and what it does not do.


19 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT REMOVES THE ACCOUNT'S DIRECTORY, OR SAYS IT DID NOT.
           Deleting an account promised to remove its directory, and usually
           did - but anything the account's own user had made without group
           write permission (a folder made at the Linux prompt, for example)
           could not be removed by the administrator, and the rest of the
           directory was left behind without a word.  The directory is now
           removed through SD's privileged helper, which accepts exactly one
           account directory and nothing else.  If one is ever still there
           afterwards, SD says so (message 10919) and names it.

           The warning before a deletion also said a suspended account
           "can be given a tier again later" - tiers are gone.  It now says
           how to let a suspended account back in:
           modify.account <name> unsuspend.

19 Sep 26  MODIFY.PASSWORD CHECKS YOUR CURRENT PASSWORD FIRST.
           When you changed your own SD password, a wrong current password
           was only noticed after you had typed the new one twice; nothing
           was changed, but the new password was asked for as though the
           old one had been accepted.  It is now checked straight away, and
           a wrong one ends the command with "Password not changed."  The
           administrator setting somebody else's password is not asked for
           the old one, as before.

19 Sep 26  YOU CAN CHANGE YOUR OWN SD PASSWORD.
           Type  MODIFY.PASSWORD  in your own account.  SD asks for your
           current SD password, then the new one twice, all hidden.  Until
           now only the administrator could set SD passwords, even your own,
           and ordinary accounts did not have the command at all.

           The rule is the same as in SD Core for Windows: you can change
           only your own password; the administrator (logged in as sdsys)
           can change anyone's, and is not asked for the old one when
           setting somebody else's.  Both of you type the same command and
           see the same prompts.

           How it is done on Linux: the password store stays readable and
           writable by the administrator only.  When you change your own
           password, SD hands the new (already scrambled) password to a
           small helper that every SD user may run through sudo for exactly
           this and nothing else; the helper checks who you are from sudo,
           checks your current password, and changes only your record.  No
           password, old or new, ever appears on a command line.

           What this does NOT do: it does not change your Linux password,
           and it does not let you change anyone else's SD password.  A
           suspended account cannot change its password.

19 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER ASKS FOR YOUR SD PASSWORD AGAIN - AND IT IS REQUIRED.
           This reverses the 18 Sep entry below, the same night, for a
           reason that entry missed.  Logging in to Linux does reach your SD
           account at the machine - but every account except sdsys can also
           be reached remotely through the SD API, and that needs an SD
           password.  The installer's own account was the one account an
           install made without one, so the remote route it opened could not
           be used.

           The installer now asks for it near the end, hidden, twice.  It is
           NOT your Linux password and does not replace it: at the machine
           and over ssh you still sign in with your Linux password.  This one
           is only for programs that reach SD through its API, from this
           computer or another.

           If you leave the prompt without setting one, the installer asks
           again, up to three times, then says in red how to set it later
           (log in as sdsys and type  MODIFY.PASSWORD  with your user name).
           It cannot force you, and it says so rather than pretend.  A
           reinstall that keeps your accounts keeps your password and does
           not ask.  SD Core for Windows made the same change the same night.

           Also: two MODIFY.PASSWORD refusals still told you to use
           "su - sdsys", which SD now refuses; they now say to log in as
           sdsys at the machine.

19 Sep 26  YOUR SD ACCOUNT IS NAMED IN LOWER CASE EVEN IF YOUR LINUX LOGIN IS
           NOT.  The installer makes an SD account for the person who
           installs SD, on top of the Linux login they already have.  If
           that login had capital letters in it - "Don" rather than "don" -
           the account's register entry was lower case but its directory
           and its sdu_ group kept the capitals, so the three did not
           match.  Now all three are lower case; the files still belong to
           your Linux login, spelled as it is.

           The installer's internal step for this is now called ATTACH, the
           same name and the same command line SD Core for Windows uses.
           ATTACH cannot be typed at an SD prompt: it works only inside the
           installer, once, for the one person installing.

           What this does NOT do: it does not rename an account made by an
           earlier install.

18 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NO LONGER ASKS FOR AN SD PASSWORD FOR YOUR ACCOUNT.
           This reverses the 15 Sep entry below.  The account the installer
           makes for you is reached by logging in to Linux and running sd -
           you have already given a password to get that far, so a second
           one bought nothing.  SD Core for Windows made the same change the
           same day, so the two installers now behave alike.

           The SD password still exists, and is still what programs using
           the SD API log in with.  If you need one, the administrator sets
           it: log in as sdsys and type  MODIFY.PASSWORD  with your user
           name.  A reinstall that keeps your accounts keeps any password
           already set.

           What this does NOT change: the installer still asks for the
           Linux password of the sdsys account, because logging in as sdsys
           is how SD is administered.

18 Sep 26  THE ACCOUNT MODEL IS NOW ONE ADMINISTRATOR AND ONE LEVEL.
           The tiered account structure - STANDARD, PROGRAMMER and
           ADMINISTRATOR, each with its own set of verbs - is gone.  Every
           account gets the same VOC: the full NEWVOC, which now also
           includes SH (the operating-system shell command) and !.  SH and
           OS.EXECUTE run at your own Linux permissions, with no SD gate
           behind them.

           There is ONE administrator, SDSYS.  It is not a rank that people
           are promoted into: it is the sdsys operating-system user, and SD
           gives it administration only when someone LOGS IN as sdsys, with
           its own password, at the machine itself (its keyboard, or a
           desktop-sharing view of it) and runs sd - not over ssh, not over
           the API from another machine, not by sudo or su from another
           user, and NOT by running sd as root, which is refused outright.  Every account except SDSYS can reach SD
           over ssh (ssh just starts SD) and through the API.

           The account verbs that moved people between levels (GRANT,
           REVOKE, LIST.GRANTS) are gone; giving a person access to an
           account is now the Linux group: usermod -aG sdu_<account> <user>.
           Suspending an account is unchanged in effect - MODIFY.ACCOUNT
           <account> SUSPENDED, and UNSUSPEND brings it back - but it is
           now a flag on the account rather than a level change.

           What this does NOT do: it does not remove the ssh boundary (SD
           accounts still get SD instead of a shell over ssh, and the sdsys
           account cannot log in over the network at all), and it does not
           change who can read or write an account's files - those limits
           are Linux's own.

18 Sep 26  A BOUND IS PUT ON TWO NAME COPIES IN THE CLEANUP PROGRAM.
           clopts (the "sd -cleanup" bookkeeping) copied a user name from
           the shared system segment into a small buffer without a bound;
           both copies now copy at most the buffer's length and always end
           with a terminator.  Reported by the Windows port; could have
           crashed the cleanup in a damaged shared segment.

15 Sep 26  ONE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE NOW NAMES THE CONFIGURATION FILE, FOR
           SD ITSELF AND FOR PROGRAMS THAT USE THE CLIENT LIBRARY.

           SD keeps its settings in /etc/sd.conf.  If you need it somewhere
           else - a test system beside a live one, say - you point SD at it
           with an environment variable, and that variable is SD_CONFIG.

           It used to be two variables that nobody could have guessed apart.
           SD itself read SCARLET_CONFIG, while the client library programs
           link against read SD_CONFIG, so setting either one moved half of
           your system to the new file and left the other half reading
           /etc/sd.conf - with no error to tell you.  SCARLET_CONFIG is no
           longer read at all; it was named after a project SD is not part
           of.

           WHAT TO DO: if you set SCARLET_CONFIG anywhere - a shell profile,
           a service file, a script - change it to SD_CONFIG.  If you have
           never set either, nothing changes: the default is still
           /etc/sd.conf, and it is used exactly as before.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not let SD find a configuration file
           on its own, and it does not change where the installer puts one.


15 Sep 26  REINSTALLING AND KEEPING YOUR ACCOUNTS NOW KEEPS THE SERVER'S
           NETWORK IDENTITY TOO.

           When a program connects to SD over the network, the connection is
           encrypted using an identity the server makes for itself the first
           time one arrives.  Until now, reinstalling threw that identity away
           even when you chose to keep your accounts, so the machine came back
           with all your accounts and passwords but a brand new identity.

           It is now kept whenever your accounts are kept, and removed only
           when you delete everything.  The delete script says which it did.

           WHY IT MATTERS EVEN THOUGH NOTHING COMPLAINS TODAY: no client
           program currently checks the server's identity, so nothing notices
           it changing.  When one does, a routine upgrade would otherwise look
           exactly like someone impersonating your server - and a warning that
           appears after every upgrade is one people learn to ignore.


15 Sep 26  DELETING AN ACCOUNT NO LONGER STOPS BECAUSE SOME OTHER ACCOUNT
           COULD NOT BE CHECKED.

           Before deleting an account, SD looks through every other account for
           references to the one going, and tells you about any it finds.  If
           one of those other accounts could not be opened - its directory had
           been removed, say - the whole command stopped with an error, before
           it had even asked you to confirm.  A check that only produces a
           warning was stopping the thing you had asked for.

           SD now names the account it could not look inside, says it was
           skipped, and gets on with the deletion.

           SD Core for Windows found this one and has the same change.


15 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER ENDS BY ASKING FOR YOUR SD PASSWORD.

           Programs that connect to SD through its API log in with an SD
           password.  It is separate from your Linux login password and is set
           with MODIFY.PASSWORD.  A new install used to finish with no SD
           password set for anyone, so nothing could use the API until an
           administrator set one by hand.

           The installer now asks for it at the end, for the account it makes
           for you.  It asks twice, to catch a typing slip.  Press Enter on an
           empty line to skip it; you can set one later by running  sudo sd
           and typing  MODIFY.PASSWORD  with your user name.  The install's
           closing summary says whether it was set, and how to set it if it
           was not.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not ask again when you reinstall and
           keep your accounts - the password you already set is kept.  And it
           does not ask for a password for SDSYS, which SD Core for Windows
           does: on Linux nothing would use one, because signing in never asks
           for an SD password and SDSYS cannot use the API.


15 Sep 26  REMOTE.API LOCAL AND OFF REMOVE THE FIREWALL RULE EVEN WHILE THE
           FIREWALL IS SWITCHED OFF.

           REMOTE.API LOCAL and REMOTE.API OFF are meant to take away the
           firewall rule that lets other computers reach port 4243.  On a
           machine where the ufw firewall was installed but not switched on,
           they left that rule in place and reported that there was none: SD
           asked ufw which rules were in force, and a switched-off ufw answers
           "none".  Nothing was exposed while ufw stayed off, but switching it
           on later would have opened port 4243 while SD said it was closed.

           SD now asks ufw which rules are configured, whether or not the
           firewall is running.  LOCAL and OFF remove the rule, ON finds it,
           and REMOTE.API and REMOTE.SSH report the rules that are really
           there.  REMOTE.SSH's warning about other rules that allow ssh reads
           the same list.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not switch the firewall on.  While ufw
           is off, the listener alone decides who can reach the API, and
           REMOTE.API reports the firewall as inactive.


15 Sep 26  THE API IS ENCRYPTED: EVERY CONNECTION IS TLS 1.3.

           Until now only the API login was protected.  The password never
           crossed the network, but everything after it did - every command,
           every record read or written, every result - as plain text that
           anyone able to see the traffic could read.  That was the weakness
           of the old QM-style API, and this closes it.

           Every connection to the SD API, over the network port (4243) and
           over the local socket alike, is now encrypted with TLS 1.3 before
           SD sends a single byte.  The login is also bound to that encrypted
           connection.  A machine placed between a program and the server
           cannot log in on the program's behalf or read what passes, even
           by pretending to be the server: the program's proof of its password
           is only good on the connection the program itself set up, and the
           program will not go on unless the server proves that it knows the
           account too.

           THE CLIENT LIBRARY (sdclilib, libsdcli) AND THE BASIC !sdclient
           CLASS do all of this automatically.  A program built against an
           older copy of the library cannot connect: the server waits for
           encryption to begin and closes the connection after ten seconds.
           Rebuild or relink it against the current library.

           The server's key is made on the first API connection, in
           /etc/sd-tls, and only root can read it.  The part of SD that
           handles the encrypted connection gives up root before it reads
           anything from the network and runs as the user nobody, so a flaw
           in it does not hand anyone root.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: programs do not yet check the server's
           certificate.  The bound login is what stops a machine in the middle
           from getting in or reading data, but such a machine could still
           collect a login attempt and try to guess the password offline.
           Use SD passwords that are long and not used anywhere else.

           Installing SD now needs the libssl-dev package, which the installer
           installs.


14 Sep 26  REMOTE.API AND REMOTE.SSH: CHANGE REMOTE ACCESS WITHOUT REINSTALLING.

           The installer asks whether other computers may reach this one over
           ssh and over the API, and until now the only way to change your
           answer was to install again.  Two administrator commands now do
           it, as they do in SD Core for Windows:

               remote.api on | local | off
               remote.ssh on | off

           REMOTE.API ON lets other computers connect to the SD API, LOCAL
           lets only this computer connect, and OFF turns the API off
           altogether.  REMOTE.SSH ON and OFF open and close port 22 in the
           firewall.  Either command with no word reports what the machine is
           doing now - read from the machine, not remembered.

           Unlike SD Core for Windows, changing the API setting does not
           restart SD or end anybody's session.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: REMOTE.SSH never starts or stops the ssh
           server itself, and on a machine whose firewall does not decide who
           reaches ssh (ufw not installed, not active, or letting everything
           in) it refuses and says so, because changing a rule there would
           change nothing.  Accounts still need an SD password, and for the
           API a grant (modify.account <account> api), whatever these say.


14 Sep 26  A FAILED OPENSEQ NO LONGER LEAVES ITS RECORD LOCKED.

           When OPENSEQ could not open a file the way it was asked - for
           example OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE on a file you may only read, which SD
           now rightly reports as an error instead of pretending to empty it -
           the lock SD had taken on that file was never released.  It stayed
           after the program ended and after the session logged off, and
           nothing but restarting SD removed it; the next program to open the
           same file waited for a lock nobody held.

           The lock is now released whenever OPENSEQ fails.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: a successful OPENSEQ keeps its lock until
           CLOSESEQ, exactly as before.


14 Sep 26  THE API IS GRANTED PER ACCOUNT.

           Until now any SD user could log in through the API once they had
           an SD password.  The API now admits only accounts that have been
           given it.  An administrator always has it.  Anyone else gets it
           with

               modify.account <account> api

           and loses it with

               modify.account <account> none

           A refused login is told "<user> is not permitted to use the API"
           and the refusal is written to the audit trail.  SD Core for
           Windows works the same way.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT now asks for the decision up front: a new account
           that is not an administrator must say API or NONE, and nothing is
           created until it does.  Taking an administrator down to a lower
           tier must also say which it keeps, for example

               modify.account <account> programmer api

           Your own administrator account keeps the API: the installer adds
           every administrator to the new sdapi group.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: logging in at a terminal or over ssh.  ssh
           still follows the account's tier, so SSH and BOTH - the other two
           words SD Core for Windows accepts - are refused here with an
           explanation rather than quietly treated as API.


14 Sep 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR CANNOT USE THE API FROM ANOTHER MACHINE.

           The API now refuses an administrator's login unless the
           connection comes from this machine - 127.0.0.1 or the local
           socket.  An administrator's Linux user can reach root through SD's
           own tools, and the API admits a connection on the SD password
           alone, so an administrator login from the network was one password
           away from the whole machine.  SD Core for Windows has the same
           rule.  The refusal says what happened and what to do instead, and
           is written to the audit trail with the address it came from.

           "Administrator" means an account at the ADMINISTRATOR tier, or a
           Linux user in the sdadmin group, whichever is true.

           WHAT STILL WORKS: an administrator on this machine, including a
           local program connecting as one; standard and programmer accounts
           from anywhere the API is reachable; and an administrator from
           elsewhere through an ssh tunnel (ssh -L), which needs the account's
           Linux login before it gets as far as SD.

           ALSO: SD now records the address a TCP API connection came FROM.
           It used to record the server's own address - the one the client
           connected to - which says nothing about who connected.


14 Sep 26  THE APILOGIN SETTING IS RETIRED, WITH THE OLD API LOGIN IT CONTROLLED.

           APILOGIN in sd.conf chose how the old cleartext API login checked
           a connection: against the Linux password (1), or not at all for a
           local-socket caller, who was simply run as whichever Linux user had
           connected (0).  That login is gone - the API accepts only SCRAM,
           described below - so the setting has nothing left to choose, and
           the code behind both choices is removed from SD.

           CONFIG no longer lists APILOGIN, and a fresh sd.conf no longer
           contains it.  An sd.conf that still has the line keeps working: SD
           reads it and ignores it, so a kept configuration does not stop SD
           starting.

           SD no longer needs the libbsd library to build, and no longer links
           the system password-hashing library (libcrypt): the only thing that
           used either was the removed login.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the API's local socket (/tmp/sdsys/
           sdclient.socket) still works, including through an ssh tunnel, but
           a program connecting over it now logs in with SCRAM and the SD
           password exactly as over the network - it is no longer let in on
           the strength of which Linux user opened it.

14 Sep 26  THE API NO LONGER ACCEPTS A PASSWORD SENT IN CLEAR.

           Programs that connect to SD through its API used to send the
           account's password across the network as it was typed.  The API
           now accepts only a SCRAM-SHA-256 login, which proves the password
           without sending it, and in which the server must prove itself to
           the program as well - so a program cannot be fooled into logging
           in to something pretending to be SD.  This is the login SD Core for
           Windows uses.

           The client library shipped with SD (sdclilib), and the BASIC
           !sdclient class, already speak it.  A program built against an
           older copy of the library is refused with the message "Cleartext
           login is no longer supported", and needs the current library.

           THE PASSWORD IS THE SD PASSWORD, SET WITH MODIFY.PASSWORD (under
           sudo sd), not the Linux login password.

           SDConnectUDS, which connected over a local socket without a
           password, is removed from the client library: it depended on the
           old login.  Connect to 127.0.0.1 with SDConnect instead.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: logging in at a terminal or over ssh, which
           still uses the Linux password; and SDConnectLocal, which starts a
           session of your own and sends no password.

14 Sep 26  THE SD CLIENT LIBRARY NO LONGER SENDS YOUR PASSWORD.

           A program that connects to SD through the client library used to
           send the user's password across the network in plain text.  It now
           logs in with SCRAM-SHA-256, as SD Core for Windows does: it proves
           it knows the password without ever sending it, and it also checks
           that the server it reached really holds the account's credential,
           so a program pretending to be SD cannot collect passwords.

           THE PASSWORD IT USES IS THE SD PASSWORD, set with MODIFY.PASSWORD
           under sudo sd, not the Linux password.  An account with no SD
           password cannot be reached through the client library until one is
           set.  The library gains no dependency: it is still one file.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the library's functions and their arguments,
           so existing programs need no change - only the password they are
           given.  The old plain-text login is still accepted by the server
           for now; it will be removed in a later update.

14 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW REMOVES THE ACCOUNT'S SD PASSWORD, AND
           MODIFY.PASSWORD SAYS STRAIGHT AWAY WHEN IT CANNOT RUN.

           Deleting an account left its SD password behind.  An account
           created later under the same name would have inherited it - the
           old password would have let someone into the new account through
           the API.  The password is now removed with the account.

           MODIFY.PASSWORD run without sudo sd asked for a new password twice
           before failing, because the password store is readable only by
           root.  It now says at once that it needs sudo sd, and asks for
           nothing.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: who may set a password, and how it is done
           under sudo sd.

14 Sep 26  MODIFY.PASSWORD NOW SETS SD'S OWN PASSWORD, AS ON WINDOWS.

           MODIFY.PASSWORD used to change the account's Linux password.  It
           now sets the password SD keeps for the account itself, the one the
           SD API is moving over to, as SD Core for Windows already does.  SD
           stores it only as keys derived from it, never as the password.
           Changing your own asks for the current one; changing another
           account's needs an administrator, and both need sudo sd, because
           the store is readable only by root.  A reinstall that keeps your
           accounts keeps these passwords too.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE YET: logging in through the API still checks
           the Linux password, until the next stage of this work.  The Linux
           password is set as before, by CREATE.ACCOUNT or with passwd.

14 Sep 26  A LONG PASSWORD NOW WORKS THROUGH THE API.

           A program connecting through the API could not use a password
           longer than 32 characters: the client library refused it as
           "Invalid password", and the server would have cut it short anyway.
           Linux puts no such limit on passwords, and neither does SD Core
           for Windows, so anyone with a long password was shut out of the
           API.  Both limits are gone.  An empty user name or password is now
           refused by the client straight away, as it is on Windows.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: user names are still limited to 32
           characters, and the password is still checked against Linux's own.

14 Sep 26  A PROGRAM CONNECTED THROUGH THE API NOW HAS THE SAME ACCESS AS ITS
           USER AT A TERMINAL.

           Linux decides what a person may read and write partly by the groups
           they belong to, and SD gives each account a group of its own.  A
           session started through the API took on the person's identity but
           none of their groups, so in an account they had been granted it
           could read but not change anything, where the same person at a
           terminal could.  The API now gives the session the person's groups
           when it logs them in.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: who may log in through the API and which
           accounts they may enter.  The session gets the groups the person
           already has in Linux and nothing more.

14 Sep 26  THE API NOW KEEPS THE SAME TIER RULE AS LOGTO.

           A grant may go down or sideways, never up.  LOGTO has enforced that
           since GRANT arrived, but a program connecting through the API
           reaches its account by a different road, and that road did not
           check.  So someone granted into an account that was later promoted
           above their own tier could still enter it through the API, with
           the higher tier's whole vocabulary.  The API now refuses them, as
           LOGTO does.

           The API also names the account in lower case, as it is everywhere
           else - WHO over the API used to print it in capitals.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: logging in through the API, and entering any
           account the person may enter with LOGTO.  The API still gives the
           same short refusal for every reason an account cannot be entered,
           so a caller cannot use it to learn which accounts exist.

14 Sep 26  GRANT'S WARNING NOW SAYS WHAT HAPPENS TO A SESSION ALREADY OPEN.

           GRANT tells you the person must log out of Linux and back in.  It
           went on to say that until they did, they could be let into the
           account and then refused by the filesystem.  A session that was
           already open is not refused: it is let in, but cannot change
           anything in the account, because Linux still sees it with the
           groups it had when it started.  The message now says so.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: GRANT and REVOKE themselves, and the advice
           to log out and back in, which is still what gives full access.

14 Sep 26  ONE CRASHED SESSION CAN NO LONGER FREEZE ALL OF SD.

           SD's processes share a small set of locks.  If a session died while
           holding one - killed, or crashing - the lock was never given back,
           and every other SD session then waited for it for ever; the only
           way out was a reboot.  Now the operating system gives the lock back
           when the process that held it ends, however it ended, and a session
           that crashes gives back its locks itself before doing anything else.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: normal locking.  The one cost is the reason
           this was a decision: after such a crash the next session may find
           the shared information that lock protected half-updated, where
           before it would have found SD frozen.


14 Sep 26  TWO MORE QUESTIONS NO LONGER WAIT FOR EVER WHEN NOBODY ANSWERS.

           "Use active select list?", asked by DELETE, COPY, CD, CT, ED and
           DELETE.FILE when a select list is active, now reads (y/<n>) and
           takes Enter as N in all six commands, which stops without changing
           anything.  Before, only DELETE.FILE did; the other five asked
           again for ever, beeping, when run from a script.

           DELETE.FILE on a multifile asked "Delete all data components?",
           and neither answer left the file alone: N still deleted its
           dictionary.  It now offers a third answer, C to cancel, which
           deletes nothing, and Enter means C.  Y and N do what they did.
           This matches SD Core for Windows.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: answering Y does exactly what it did.


14 Sep 26  BACKSPACE WORKS AT THE COMMAND LINE WHICHEVER BYTE YOUR TERMINAL
           SENDS.

           A terminal's backspace key sends either Ctrl-H or DEL.  SD only
           accepted the one its terminal type said to expect, so after TERM
           VT100 (which expects Ctrl-H) the backspace key of a normal Linux
           terminal, which sends DEL, did nothing at the command line.  Both
           are now accepted as backspace unless the terminal type gives one of
           them another meaning.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the full-screen editors handle keys
           themselves and are not affected.


14 Sep 26  RUN FINDS A PROGRAM WHATEVER CASE ITS NAME IS TYPED IN.

           BASIC stores a compiled program under a lower-case name, so
           "BASIC BP REPORT" compiles to bp.out/report.  RUN did not do the
           same, so "RUN BP REPORT" then answered "Program BP.OUT REPORT not
           found" and only "RUN BP report" worked.  RUN now looks for the name
           as typed, then in lower case, then in upper case, the same order SD
           uses for file and command names.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: a program is still run from the file you
           name, and a name that matches nothing still gets the same message.


14 Sep 26  PROCESS DUMPS HAVE THEIR OWN DIRECTORY, AND ONLY THEIR OWNER CAN READ
           ONE.

           PDUMP writes a file holding a session's variables, call stack and
           open files.  It used to be aimed at the system directory: an
           ordinary user's dump could not be written there at all ("Cannot
           open dump file"), and an administrator's landed where every user
           could read it.  Dumps now go to /usr/local/sdsys/dumps.  Any SD user
           can create one there, nobody but an administrator can list the
           directory, and each dump is readable only by the user whose session
           it is (and by root).  If a dump cannot be written, the message now
           says where and why.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: who may PDUMP which process.  The DUMPDIR
           setting in /etc/sd.conf still chooses the directory; an install
           that kept its old configuration has DUMPDIR added for it.


14 Sep 26  SIGNING ON AFTER A RELEASE CHANGE NO LONGER HANGS A SCRIPT.

           When an account's VOC was at an older release, sign-on asked
           "Update VOC to new release?" and accepted only Y or N.  Pressing
           Enter asked again, and a script or pipe driving sd - which has no
           answer to give - made it ask for ever, beeping each time.  The
           prompt now reads "Update VOC to new release (y/<n>)?", and Enter,
           or the end of input, means N.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: answering Y still updates the VOC.  N leaves
           it as it was, and you are asked again at the next sign-on.


14 Sep 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR CAN LOGTO AN ACCOUNT CREATED IN THE SAME SESSION.

           In a sudo sd session, LOGTO to an account whose Linux group did not
           exist when the session started failed with "Error 3001 opening
           file at line 2952 of $CPROC" and left you where you were - for
           example, CREATE.ACCOUNT followed by LOGTO to the new account.  A
           running program keeps the group memberships it started
           with, and SD never looked again.  SD now refreshes them when the
           session starts and after each CREATE.ACCOUNT, DELETE.ACCOUNT and
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: who may enter which account, and who owns the
           files SD writes.  A group change made outside SD while a session is
           open is still only seen after one of those commands, or in a new
           session.


14 Sep 26  RUN SAYS WHEN A PROGRAM'S PATH IS TOO LONG, AND WHAT THE LIMIT IS.

           When the full path to a compiled program was longer than 128
           characters - an account kept deep in the directory tree, say - RUN
           failed with "Invalid runfile pathname", which read as though the
           name had been typed wrongly.  It now says "Runfile pathname is
           longer than 128 characters".

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the limit itself is still 128; a path that
           long still cannot be run this way.  Catalogued programs are not
           affected.


14 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NO LONGER WARNS THAT SD MAY NEED TWO REBOOTS TO START.

           Its closing message used to say that in rare cases SD would not
           start at boot until the computer had been restarted twice, or SD
           was started by hand.  That was the service fault fixed on 13 Sep
           (see "SD now stays running when the computer starts it" below), and
           SD has started and stayed running on every boot since, so the
           warning is gone.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: if SD is ever found stopped, starting it by
           hand still works - sudo /usr/local/sdsys/bin/sd -start.


13 Sep 26  THE TIER POLICY LISTS MOVED TO THEIR OWN FILE.

           The two lists that decide which verbs each account tier gets - the
           verbs a STANDARD account is denied, and the ones an ADMINISTRATOR
           gains on top - used to live in SDSYS newvoc as the records
           TIER.OMIT.STANDARD and TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR.  They are not
           vocabulary entries, so they did not belong in a VOC file - it was
           also the last thing in newvoc whose name was not lower case.

           They now live in a dedicated file, SDSYS tier.policy, as the records
           omit.standard and add.administrator.  IF YOU CUSTOMISE what a tier
           starts with, edit them there.  Nothing else changes: each tier gets
           exactly the verbs it did before.


13 Sep 26  SD'S OWN PROGRAM SOURCES AND INCLUDE RECORDS HAVE LOWER-CASE NAMES:
           gpl.bp/cproc, gpl.bp/login, syscom/keys.h, syscom/err.h and the rest.

           Every record in the system's program file gpl.bp and include file
           syscom now has a lower-case name, so SD's own names follow the same
           rule as everything else on disk.  With this, every name SD ships -
           directories, VOC entries, programs and include records - is lower
           case, apart from two tier lists that are due to move out of the VOC.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: $INCLUDE SYSCOM KEYS.H, $INCLUDE
           KEYS.H and BASIC GPL.BP CPROC all still work, because the compiler
           finds an include or source record in either case.  Catalogued
           names - the ones CALL uses, such as !PARSER - are untouched.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: RUN takes a program's name exactly, so a program
           in gpl.bp is now run as RUN gpl.bp write_install_dicts, not in
           capitals.  A script that reads these files by their path -
           /usr/local/sdsys/syscom/KEYS.H - needs the lower-case name.


13 Sep 26  WHAT YOU TYPE IS NO LONGER CASE-FLIPPED IN AN ACCOUNT WITHOUT ITS
           LOGIN PARAGRAPH.

           SD started every session with "case inversion" on - typing list
           gave LIST and typing LIST gave list - and relied on each account's
           login paragraph (PTERM CASE NOINVERT) to turn it off again.  An
           account whose login paragraph had been removed or replaced typed
           with every letter flipped, which quietly breaks anything that takes
           a name exactly, such as COPY FROM VOC or a record id.

           Case inversion now starts off.  Nothing changes in an ordinary
           account, where it was already being turned off.

           TO USE CASE INVERSION deliberately, PTERM CASE INVERT still turns it
           on, for a session or in a login paragraph.


13 Sep 26  ACCOUNT NAMES ARE LOWER CASE, THE SYSTEM ACCOUNT INCLUDED: don, sdsys.

           An account's name is now stored in lower case, so it matches the
           Linux user it belongs to.  The system account is sdsys, the same as
           its Linux user.  @WHO, WHO and WHO.AM.I show the lower-case name.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: LOGTO SDSYS, LOGTO DON,
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT DON ..., DELETE.ACCOUNT, GRANT, SET.FILE and
           MODIFY.PASSWORD all accept the name in any case, as does a
           Q-pointer that names an account.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: a program that compares @WHO with a name in
           capitals - IF @WHO = 'SDSYS' - needs the lower-case name now.  And
           an account register from an earlier release has upper-case names
           and will not work with this one; there are none outside
           development, so delete and re-create any test installation.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: account directories were already lower case,
           and the names of Linux users and groups are untouched.


13 Sep 26  S AT A REPORT'S PAGE PROMPT NOW SCROLLS THE REST OF THE REPORT.

           Answering S ("Suppress pagination") at the "Action (...)" prompt of
           LIST, SORT and the other report commands looked as though it ended
           the report.  It did not: every remaining record was sent, but SD
           still cleared the screen and redrew the heading at every page, so
           on a real terminal the pages flashed past and only the last one was
           left on screen with the record count beneath it.

           S now behaves exactly as if the report had been given NO.PAGE from
           that point: the rest of the report scrolls up the terminal without
           clearing the screen or repeating the heading.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: RETURN or N still shows the next page, Q still
           quits and A still aborts, and a report sent to a printer or to a
           hold file was never affected.


13 Sep 26  THE LAST UPPER-CASE VOC ENTRIES SD SHIPS ARE LOWER CASE: $acc, $map,
           $release, and $command.stack in every new account.

           $release records the release your VOC is at, $acc points at the
           account's own directory, $map is where MAP.CAT writes, and
           $command.stack is the marker that tells SD to keep your command
           history between sessions.  With these, every VOC entry SD ships or
           creates is lower case.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: COUNT $ACC, LIST $MAP and
           MAP.CAT FILE $MAP work as before.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: SD looks for $release and $command.stack by their
           exact names when you sign in and sign off.  An account made before
           this release has them in upper case and would be refused at sign-in
           with "$release VOC record not found" - there are no such accounts
           outside development, so delete and re-create any test account.


13 Sep 26  THE SDSYS REGISTER RECORD IS INSTALLED WITH THE PERMISSIONS INTENDED.

           The installer meant the system account's register record to be
           readable but not writable by anyone but root, and set that - and
           then two later steps undid it, so it was installed with execute
           permission as well.  It is now set last, to read/write for root and
           read for everyone else, like every other account's record, and the
           installer prints what it set.


13 Sep 26  CREATE.FILE NOW NAMES A NEW FILE IN LOWER CASE, WHATEVER CASE YOU TYPE.

           CREATE.FILE ORDERS makes a file called orders: the VOC entry is
           orders, the directory on disk is orders and its dictionary is
           orders.dic.  Before this, the VOC entry kept the case you typed and
           the directory was always made in upper case, so on Linux, where a
           name's case is part of the name, CREATE.FILE ORDERS and
           CREATE.FILE orders could leave two different files.  A file now has
           one name.

           If a file of that name already exists in any case, CREATE.FILE
           finds it rather than making a second one.  DELETE.FILE ORDERS now
           finds orders without asking, and so does SET.FILE when it looks in
           another account.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: LIST ORDERS, COUNT ORDERS and
           OPEN "ORDERS" in a program all find orders, as they already found
           names in either case.  The compiled-program file BASIC makes is
           now bp.out on disk as well.

           TO USE THE NAME EXACTLY AS TYPED instead, set the option whose
           description is "Keep operating system file name case in
           CREATE.FILE" - it is listed by OPTION under the historical name
           CREATE.FILE.UPCASE, which is misleading: it keeps case, it does not
           upper-case.  With it set, CREATE.FILE uses the name you typed for
           the VOC entry and on disk.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: files that already exist keep their names, and
           the records inside a file keep theirs - SUE and sue are still two
           records.


13 Sep 26  EVERY ACCOUNT'S OWN FILES ARE NOW LOWER CASE ON DISK: voc, $hold,
           $hold.dic, $svlists and bp.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT now makes these names, and the system account's own
           voc and $hold follow them, so the directory SD makes for your VOC
           is voc and your BASIC programs go in bp.  The VOC entry for your
           program file is bp too.  These are the names SD Core for Windows
           uses.  Printing to the hold file writes into $hold.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: BASIC BP MYPROG, RUN BP MYPROG,
           SP.VIEW and GET.LIST all work as before, because names are found
           through the VOC in either case.

           FIXED ON THE WAY - "Data pathname 'BP.OUT' already exists".  If the
           first program you compiled in an account was typed with the file
           name in lower case (BASIC bp MYPROG), SD made its compiled-program
           file under a mixed-case name, bp.OUT, that it could never find
           again; every later BASIC BP stopped with that message until the
           file was deleted by hand.  The compiled-program file is now named
           from the file's VOC entry, so typing bp gives bp.out, and BASIC BP
           afterwards finds it.

           WHAT IS NOT CHANGED YET: CREATE.FILE still makes a new file's
           directory in upper case, so the compiled-program directory on disk
           is still BP.OUT behind the bp.out VOC entry.  That is the next
           change.  An account made before this one keeps its old names and
           will not work with this release - there are no such accounts
           outside development, so delete and re-create any test account.


13 Sep 26  THE SYSTEM'S PROGRAM DIRECTORIES ARE NOW LOWER CASE TOO: gpl.bp,
           gpl.bp.out, bp, bp.out and pcode.out.

           The second half of the move that renamed the data directories
           earlier today.  gpl.bp holds SD's own BASIC source and gpl.bp.out
           its compiled programs; bp and bp.out are the system account's own
           program files.  The system account's VOC entries now name them in
           lower case, which is also how SD Core for Windows names them.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: BASIC GPL.BP CPROC and
           $INCLUDE GPL.BP HEADER.H still work, because the file is found
           through the VOC in either case.  The programs inside keep their
           names - CPROC is still CPROC.

           WHAT IS NOT RENAMED YET: the BP file in YOUR account, which
           CREATE.ACCOUNT still makes as BP, and every account's own VOC and
           $HOLD.  Those move together, separately.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: a script that reaches SD's source by its path -
           /usr/local/sdsys/GPL.BP/CPROC, say - needs the lower-case name.


13 Sep 26  SD NOW STAYS RUNNING WHEN THE COMPUTER STARTS IT.

           Some of the time SD would start at boot and then be shut down again
           a second later, so the first person to type sd was told "SD has not
           been started".  The system service manager was watching the wrong
           process: when SD's background process set itself up, the service
           manager saw a short-lived helper finish, decided SD had stopped, and
           stopped it.  The service now counts SD as running once
           "sd -start" says it has started, and leaves it running until it is
           asked to stop.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: how SD is started and stopped by hand
           (sudo /usr/local/sdsys/bin/sd -start and -stop), and what it does
           once running.  systemctl status sd now shows "active (exited)"
           while SD is up; that is expected, and means the service is
           running, not that it has ended.


13 Sep 26  THE SYSTEM'S DATA DIRECTORIES ARE NOW LOWER CASE ON DISK.

           Under /usr/local/sdsys, the directories that hold SD's own data now
           have lower-case names: accounts, newvoc, voc_template, messages,
           syscom and sd.voclib, and the files the install creates - $ipc,
           $map, $map.dic, voc.dic, accounts.dic, dict.dic and dir_dict.  The
           VOC entries that point at them name the new paths, so
           CT VOC syscom now shows "2: @SDSYS/syscom".  These are the names SD
           Core for Windows uses.

           Why it matters: on Linux a file name's case is part of the name, so
           SYSCOM and syscom could otherwise both exist.  Giving each system
           directory one spelling, and the same spelling as the VOC entry, is
           a step towards SD having no name in two casings.

           WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE: COUNT SYSCOM and $INCLUDE SYSCOM
           KEYS.H work as before, because the VOC entry is found in either
           case.

           WHAT IS NOT RENAMED YET: GPL.BP, BP and PCODE.OUT, and the system
           account's own VOC and $HOLD, move separately.  The records inside
           these directories keep their names: the register still holds
           SDSYS, and syscom still holds KEYS.H.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: a script or program of your own that reaches one
           of these directories by its path - /usr/local/sdsys/ACCOUNTS, say -
           needs the lower-case name.  Anything that goes through the VOC is
           unaffected.


12 Sep 26  THE SYSTEM FILE POINTERS ARE NOW LOWER CASE IN THE VOC: voc, syscom,
           newvoc, dict.dict, md, sd.accounts, sd.voclib, and in the system
           account accounts, messages and qfile.

           As with the commands, what you type does not change: LIST VOC,
           COUNT SYSCOM and $INCLUDE SYSCOM KEYS.H all work as before, and
           CT VOC SYSCOM now answers with the heading "VOC syscom".

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: the files themselves.  These are only the VOC
           entries' names; the directories on disk (SYSCOM, NEWVOC and the
           rest) keep their current names for now.

           THE SAME CAUTION AS BEFORE: a command that takes a record id exactly
           - COPY FROM VOC SYSCOM,MYPTR, or QSELECT VOC SYSCOM - now needs the
           lower-case id.  Names you open or type are found in either case.


12 Sep 26  SD'S COMMANDS ARE NOW LOWER CASE IN THE VOC: list, create.file,
           count, and the other 380 or so.

           Every verb, keyword, paragraph, phrase and sentence SD ships is now
           stored under a lower-case name.  WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE:
           LIST, list and List all run the same command, as they already did.
           What you will notice is what SD prints back - CT VOC LIST now
           answers with a heading of "VOC list".

           WHAT IS NOT RENAMED YET: file pointers (VOC, BP, QFILE, SYSCOM and
           the rest), and the $ records ($ACC, $MAP, $RELEASE).  Those move
           separately.

           ONE THING TO KNOW: a command that reads a VOC record by its exact
           name - COPY FROM VOC LIST,MYLIST, for example - now needs the
           lower-case name (COPY FROM VOC list,MYLIST).  Commands you TYPE are
           found in any case; a record id handed to COPY is taken as given.


12 Sep 26  THE HOLD FILE IS NOW CALLED $hold.

           The second system name to move to lower case.  The VOC entry for the
           file that holds deferred prints - where SETPTR mode 3 sends output,
           and what SP.VIEW reads - is now $hold, and SD prints that spelling:
           SETPTR 5 now shows "Hold file: $hold P5" rather than "$HOLD P5".

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: your held prints.  Only the VOC entry's name
           moved; the directory on disk that holds them is still $HOLD.


12 Sep 26  THE SAVED-LIST FILE IS NOW CALLED $savedlists, THE FIRST SYSTEM NAME
           TO MOVE TO LOWER CASE.

           SD's system names are moving to lower case, one group at a time.
           This is the first: the VOC entry for the file that SAVE.LIST,
           GET.LIST, DELETE.LIST, COPY.LIST, EDIT.LIST and SAVE.STACK use is
           now $savedlists.  A new account gets it under that name.

           You can still type $SAVEDLISTS: names are found in either case.

           WHAT DOES NOT CHANGE: your saved lists.  Only the name of the VOC
           entry moved; the directory that holds the lists is where it was,
           with everything in it.


12 Sep 26  A NAME NOW FINDS ITS VOC ENTRY WHETHER YOU TYPE IT IN UPPER OR
           LOWER CASE.

           SD has always forgiven a command typed in lower case: type "who" and
           it tries WHO.  It never forgave the other direction.  A VOC entry
           whose name is lower case could only be reached by typing it in lower
           case, so:

               CREATE.FILE orders
               COUNT orders       0 record(s) counted
               COUNT ORDERS       File not found

           CREATE.FILE had just made that entry, and the name it printed back
           was the one COUNT then could not find.  The same was true of a verb
           you saved under a lower-case name, and of a program that opens a
           file by name.

           Every place SD looks a name up now tries it exactly as typed, then
           in lower case, then in upper case - the commands you type, the
           query language (LIST, COUNT, SELECT and their keywords), BASIC's
           OPEN and OPENSEQ, a Q-pointer's target, RUN, the stacked-command
           editor, and the compiler's $INCLUDE.  An entry spelled exactly as
           you typed it still wins, so nothing that works today changes.

           This is the groundwork for SD's system names becoming lower case,
           which is still to come; it has to be in place first, or that change
           would stop every upper-case command from working.

           WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not make the query language's RECORD
           ids case-insensitive.  LIST CUSTOMERS "SUE" still does not find a
           record called "sue" - on Linux those can be two different records,
           and SD will not guess between them.  (CT and ED already fell back to
           the upper-case id when the one you typed was not there; they now
           try lower case too, as they do for names.)


12 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW FULLY REMOVES AN ADMINISTRATOR IT DELETES, EVEN
           WHEN THE LINUX USER STAYS.

           Most SD accounts own the Linux user behind them - SD created it, and
           DELETE.ACCOUNT removes it along with everything else.  But an account
           that was ADOPTED onto a Linux user SD did not create (the person who
           installed SD is the usual one) leaves that Linux user in place when
           the account is deleted, because the login and its files are not SD's
           to destroy.

           Until now, deleting such an account left that Linux user in SD's
           administrator group.  On Linux that group grants real power - a
           member can run SD's privileged helper without a password - so an
           administrator whose account you had deleted could still act as one,
           and from there reach the root account.  "I deleted that
           administrator" did not fully mean it.

           DELETE.ACCOUNT now strips the SD groups it granted - administrator
           first, then the general SD-user group - from a surviving Linux user,
           so a deleted account confers nothing afterwards.  The Linux user and
           its home are still left untouched; only the memberships SD itself
           added are removed.  Deleting an account whose Linux user SD created
           is unchanged: that user, and everything with it, still goes.


12 Sep 26  DELETE.FILE: PRESSING ENTER AT "USE ACTIVE SELECT LIST?" NOW MEANS
           NO.

           If you have a select list active and type DELETE.FILE with no file
           name, it offers to use the list:

               Use active select list (First item 'PAYROLL')?

           Pressing Enter used to do nothing at all - the question simply came
           back, for ever, until you typed Y or N.  In a script or a piped
           session there is nothing to type, so it never stopped: the same
           question scrolled past until something killed it.

           Enter now means N, which clears the list and deletes nothing.  That
           is the safe answer, and it matches the rest of this verb - every
           other question DELETE.FILE asks already takes N when you press
           Enter.

           THE QUESTION ITSELF STILL DOES NOT SAY SO, and that is deliberate
           rather than an oversight: the same wording is used by DELETE, CD,
           COPY, CT and ED, none of which takes a default yet, so adding
           "(y/<n>)" to it would be wrong in five places to be right in one.
           Those verbs are unchanged - in them, Enter still re-asks.


12 Sep 26  DELETE.FILE NO.QUERY NO LONGER ASKS A QUESTION AFTER PROMISING NOT
           TO.

           Deleting a file whose data lives in the system account - a VOC entry
           pointing at @SDSYS/something - does the safe thing under NO.QUERY:
           the VOC reference goes and the shared file is left alone, and it
           tells you so.  That part was right.  What followed it was not.  The
           verb then went on to ask

               OK to delete DATA portion '' (y/<n>)?

           three times, about a file with no name, after NO.QUERY had already
           been honoured.  Typed at a terminal it was a puzzling question you
           could answer with Enter.  In a script or a piped session it was
           worse: the prompt swallowed the next lines of input as its answers,
           so the commands after DELETE.FILE never ran and nothing said why.

           The right thing still happened underneath - the VOC entry went, the
           shared file stayed - which is exactly why this went unnoticed.  Only
           the route was wrong.

           IT DOES NOT CHANGE WHAT GETS DELETED.  NO.QUERY still declines to
           remove a file out of the system account, and still tells you the
           file is still there; delete it without NO.QUERY to be asked about
           the file itself.


12 Sep 26  MODIFY.PASSWORD SETS AN ACCOUNT'S PASSWORD FROM INSIDE SD.

               modify.password           - your own
               modify.password bob       - somebody else's (administrators)

           An SD account is a Linux user here, so its password is that user's
           Linux password: there is no second password to get out of step with
           the real one.

           SD NEVER SEES THE PASSWORD.  It does not prompt for one, hold one,
           or pass one to anything - it hands over to passwd, which does its
           own prompting.  And a password is never accepted on the command
           line: "modify.password bob hunter2" is refused rather than quietly
           ignoring the second word, because by the time you saw a prompt the
           password would already be in SD's command stack.

           Setting your own asks for your current one - Linux asks, not SD, so
           it follows whatever password rules this machine already has.
           Setting somebody else's needs administrator rights and does not ask
           for the old one, because an administrator resetting a forgotten
           password does not know it.

           ONLY ADMINISTRATOR ACCOUNTS HAVE THIS VERB, and that is a starting
           position rather than a rule about who deserves it.  SD ships its
           security tight and leaves you to loosen it deliberately: if you want
           one particular person to set their own password, copy the
           MODIFY.PASSWORD entry from VOC_TEMPLATE into that account's VOC.

           They can then change their own password and nothing else - asking
           for somebody else's is still refused, because that needs
           administrator rights and copying a verb does not confer any.  Remove
           the entry again and they are back where they were.

           Worth knowing if you do: an upgrade will not take the entry away
           again, but changing that account's tier will, because a tier change
           rebuilds the account's administrative verbs from scratch.

           What it does not do:

           It will not set a password for a group account, which has no Linux
           user of its own and so has nothing to set.

           It never touches the root account.  The user is always named
           explicitly, so the verb cannot be pointed at whoever happens to be
           running it.


11 Sep 26  UPGRADING NOW BRINGS YOUR ACCOUNTS' VOCs WITH IT.

           Installing a new release replaces the master lists SD builds new
           accounts from, but it never touched the accounts you already had.
           So every verb added since an account was created was simply not
           typeable in it, and there was no way to find out except by typing
           one and being told it was not in your VOC.

           An upgrade - a reinstall that keeps your accounts - now walks every
           registered account and brings its VOC up to the new release.

           ADMINISTRATOR ACCOUNTS GET THE ADMINISTRATOR VERBS THEY WERE
           MISSING, which is the half that had never worked at all.  Those
           verbs come from a different list, and nothing except creating the
           account or changing its tier had ever copied them.  On this very
           system the only registered administrator was missing eight of the
           eighteen - including create.account, delete.account, modify.account
           and update.accounts.

           It had gone unnoticed because administration is normally done with
           "sudo sd", which puts you in SDSYS, and SDSYS has every verb.  Sign
           on as yourself and the gaps appear.

           You can run the walk yourself at any time, from SDSYS:

               update.accounts all

           What it does not do:

           It never removes anything.  A VOC gains what the new release added
           and keeps everything it had, including records you have edited.
           Only accounts whose tier is ADMINISTRATOR receive administrator
           verbs; programmer and standard accounts are untouched by that part.

           It may ask, once per account, about a record whose type has changed
           between releases.  That question is unavoidable - SD will not
           silently replace a record of one kind with another - so an upgrade
           is worth watching rather than walking away from.


11 Sep 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NO LONGER TAKES OVER AN EXISTING LINUX USER.

           SD accounts create their own Linux user.  That has been the model
           for a while, but the verb did not enforce it: if you typed

               create.account user bob

           and a Linux user called bob already existed, SD quietly attached an
           SD account to that person's existing login - no password set, no
           account created, nothing said.  It now refuses:

               Linux user bob already exists.  SD accounts create their own
               Linux user

           Pick a name SD will create, and SD will make the Linux user, set its
           password and build the account together.

           THE ONE EXCEPTION IS THE PERSON WHO INSTALLS SD, whose Linux login
           obviously already exists.  The installer adopts it, and that is the
           only adoption the system performs.

           AND A REFUSAL THAT NAMED THE WRONG THING IS FIXED.  Asking for
           no.query when the Linux user does not exist used to answer "Invalid
           user name", which sent you off to check a name that was perfectly
           good.  The real reason is that creating a Linux user means setting
           its password, and that needs a prompt, so no.query cannot work.  It
           now says so.

           What it does not do:

           There is no way to give an existing Linux user an SD account.  That
           is deliberate, not an oversight - it is what "SD accounts create
           their own Linux user" means.  The installer's adoption is gated on
           being run from inside the installer and cannot be reached from a
           command line, so typing ADOPT yourself answers "Unexpected token".

           Existing accounts are untouched.  This changes what CREATE.ACCOUNT
           will do next time, not anything already created.


11 Sep 26  GIVING SOMEBODY ELSE ONE OF YOUR ACCOUNTS: GRANT AND REVOKE.

           An administrator can let a second person into an account, take that
           access away again, and see who has it:

               grant payroll to bob
               revoke payroll from bob
               list.grants payroll

           Entry to an account has always been membership of the Linux group
           CREATE.ACCOUNT makes for it - sdu_payroll for account payroll - so
           these verbs edit that group and change nothing in the account's
           record.  Until now there was no verb for it at all: you added and
           removed members with MODIFY.ACCOUNT, and there was no way to ask who
           was in the group without leaving SD.

           A GRANT MAY GO DOWN OR SIDEWAYS, AND NEVER UP.  A programmer can be
           given a standard account; a standard user cannot be given a
           programmer account.  Your own tier has to be at least the tier of
           the account you are being let into.  Somebody with no SD account of
           their own has no tier to compare, so they cannot be granted one.

           NOBODY IS EVER GRANTED SDSYS.  It belongs to administrators, who
           reach it by logging in or with LOGTO.  It is not something a tier
           can be raised to obtain, and the refusal says so.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT ADD OBEYS THE SAME RULE, because it makes the same
           group change and would otherwise be the way round it.  DELETE does
           not: taking access away is always allowed.

           AND A PROMOTION NOW SAYS WHAT IT BROKE.  Raising an account's tier
           can turn people who were legitimately granted it into people SD will
           now refuse - the membership still looks perfectly normal from the
           outside.  MODIFY.ACCOUNT compares who could get in before and after
           the change and lists anybody the change stopped, with what to do
           about it.  Lowering a tier cannot break a grant, so it prints
           nothing.  If the group cannot be read at all it says THAT, rather
           than leaving a silence that looks like good news.

           What it does not do:

           The person must log out of Linux and back in before they can write
           in the account.  SD sees the grant at once and will let them in; the
           account's directory belongs to the group, and Linux gives a session
           the groups it had when it started.  Until they log in again they can
           be let in and then refused by the filesystem.  Revoking has the same
           delay in reverse - SD stops admitting them immediately, but their
           access to the files lasts until their next login.

           It does not stop an administrator making the same group change with
           usermod outside SD, and no rule here can.  SD applies the tier rule
           again at LOGTO, which is what actually holds.

           LIST.GRANTS reports the members of the group, which includes the
           account's own user and root.  Neither is a grant somebody made -
           CREATE.ACCOUNT puts them there - but hiding them would make the
           listing disagree with the group it is describing.

           The three verbs are new, so an administrator account created before
           this release does not have them, and neither UPDATE.ACCOUNTS nor
           re-typing the tier it already has will add them: a tier change only
           rewrites the VOC when the tier actually moves.  An administrator
           account created after this release has them from the start.  For an
           older one, move the tier down and back up:

               modify.account bob programmer
               modify.account bob administrator

           Do that to the account you are signed in as only if another
           administrator is registered, because the first command takes your
           own administrator rights away.


11 Sep 26  SD NOW KEEPS AN AUDIT TRAIL.

           The file "audit" in /usr/local/sdsys records every sign-on, every
           sign-on that was refused and why, every LOGTO and every refused
           LOGTO, the administrator rights sudo sd gives or refuses, refused
           API logins, and account changes made with MODIFY.ACCOUNT: members
           added and removed, tier changes (suspensions included) and SH /
           OS.EXECUTE grants.  Each line has the date, the time, the Linux
           user, the SD user number and the process:

              2026-09-11 14:02:07 user=don uid=3 pid=8624 LOGTO account=TSTD

           Under sudo sd the process belongs to root, so the line also names
           who ran sudo: "user=root sudo=don".

           The refusals are the interesting half - somebody who is not an
           administrator asking for SDSYS, or for an account they are not a
           member of.

           THIS IS NOT THE ERROR LOG.  The error log throws away its oldest
           half when it fills; the audit file is renamed with the date and time
           when SD starts and finds it over 1MB, and a new one begun, so nothing
           is discarded.  Removing old ones is your decision.

           SD USERS CAN ADD TO IT AND DO NOTHING ELSE.  They cannot read it,
           change or empty it, or rename or delete it - Linux itself refuses,
           because the file gives them write without read and is append-only
           (chattr +a).  Administrators read it with sudo.  To remove an old
           one: sudo chattr -a <file>, then delete it.  If the installer says
           it could not make the file append-only, your filesystem does not
           support it, and SD users could empty the file.

           Uninstalling with your accounts kept keeps the audit trail too, and
           reinstalling puts it back.

           What it does not do: Linux keeps its own record of logins and sudo
           use (the journal, or /var/log/auth.log), separate from this one and
           more complete; read the two together.


11 Sep 26  AN ACCOUNT CAN BE SUSPENDED.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT takes a fourth tier:

               modify.account bob suspended

           A suspended account loses all access to SD: the console, ssh, LOGTO
           from another account and the API all refuse it.  It is for an
           account that should stop working for a while - somebody on leave,
           a login being investigated - without deleting anything.  If you
           only want to stop somebody using SD, suspend the account rather
           than deleting it; DELETE.ACCOUNT now says so before it deletes.

           NOTHING IN LINUX CHANGES.  The Linux user, its password and its
           groups - sdadmin included - are left exactly as they are, and so is
           the account's VOC.  SD keeps the tier the account had, LIST
           SD.ACCOUNTS shows it, and suspending says all of this at the time.

           THERE IS NO "RESUME" KEYWORD.  Coming back names the tier:

               modify.account bob programmer

           and the VOC is brought from the tier it had to the one named.  An
           account can go from any tier to any other directly.

           What it does not do: an administrator can still LOGTO a suspended
           account, to look at it.  You cannot suspend the account you logged
           in to or the one you are standing in.  A session already inside the
           account when it is suspended carries on until it ends.


11 Sep 26  A PROGRAM CAN CREATE A SEQUENTIAL FILE AGAIN.

           The normal way for a program to create a file is OPENSEQ, which
           takes its ELSE branch because the file does not exist yet, then
           WRITESEQ, then CLOSESEQ.  Since a code clean-up earlier this year,
           that did not work: OPENSEQ threw away the file variable the moment
           it handed it to the program, so every WRITESEQ was refused with
           "not a sequential file", nothing was written, and the file was
           never created.  Worse, the lock OPENSEQ had taken on the file's
           name was kept, even through CLOSESEQ and logging out, with no
           session to own it: the next OPENSEQ of the same file waited for
           ever, only restarting SD cleared it, and listing the locks at that
           point could stop SD for every user.  OPENSEQ now keeps the file
           variable, as SD always did before the clean-up and as SD Core for
           Windows does, so the file is written and the lock is released when
           it is closed or the program ends.

           What it does not do: a lock already stranded this way is not
           cleared by installing this version; restart SD once.


11 Sep 26  SD ENDS A SESSION WHEN ITS INPUT RUNS OUT, INSTEAD OF SPINNING.

           When SD reads its commands from a pipe or a file - for example
           printf 'LISTU\n' | sd, or sd < commands - and the input ran out
           without an OFF, the command prompt did not stop.  It asked for the
           next command again and again, as fast as the machine could go,
           sending a bell character each time, until something killed the
           process.  It now logs the session out, exactly as OFF would.

           What it does not do: a session at a real terminal is unchanged -
           SD still waits for you to type.  A program that uses INPUT still
           receives an empty answer at the end of its input and decides for
           itself what that means.


11 Sep 26  A SESSION THAT DIED NO LONGER LOCKS A FILE FOR EVER.

           When a command needs a file to itself - BUILD.INDEX, DELETE.INDEX,
           CNAME, CONFIGURE.FILE, SET.TRIGGER - and cannot get it, it now names
           the file and the session holding it ("Holding it: 12 (don)"), and
           says what to do.  If nothing else has it open, it says the file is
           still referenced inside your own session instead.  LOGOUT n on a
           session whose process has already gone - a dropped ssh connection, a
           closed window - now reclaims its slot and releases every lock it
           held, and says so, instead of leaving it "(logout pending)" for ever.

           LIST.READU no longer crashes on a lock whose session has gone; it
           shows the owner as "(gone)".  The crash left SD's internal locks
           held, which stopped every other session.

           What it does not do: LOGOUT n on a session that is still running
           asks it to log out, as before; you can log out only your own
           sessions unless you are an administrator.


11 Sep 26  PROTECTING A VOC RECORD YOU HAVE CHANGED.

           If you have customised a record in your account's VOC - a sentence,
           a paragraph, a keyword, a file pointer - you can now keep it through
           a VOC update by putting [locked] in its first field, after the type
           code (for example "PA [locked] my nightly run").  The update leaves
           it as it is and then lists every record it left alone, so you know
           which corrections you have not received.

           What it does not do: it does not protect a verb.  A verb is what SD
           runs, and holding one back would leave your account calling a
           program this release has changed; a verb marked [locked] is updated
           anyway and listed separately.  To change what a command does, add
           your own verb under a name SD does not ship - updates never touch
           those.

           Also: when CREATE.ACCOUNT cannot set a new user's password it now
           says why - the two entries did not match or the system's password
           rules refused it, the name is one SD may not set, or the system's
           own status number - and it no longer offers a retry that cannot
           succeed.


11 Sep 26  LISTF SAYS WHAT THE SYSTEM FILES ARE.

           Ten of the file entries in the system account's VOC - ACCOUNTS, BP,
           BP.OUT, GPL.BP, GPL.BP.OUT, MESSAGES, NEWVOC, QFILE, $HOLD and the
           NEWVOC entry given to every account - now carry a description, so
           LISTF reads "File - Account register" or "File - BASIC program
           source" instead of a bare F.

           What it does not do: nothing about how these files are found or
           opened changes; SD reads only the first letter of that field as the
           type, as before.


11 Sep 26  CSV FILES FROM WINDOWS READ CORRECTLY.

           READSEQ now treats a CR LF pair as the end of a line, as it already
           treated LF alone.  A file written on Windows, or any CSV file that
           follows the CSV standard (which uses CR LF even on Linux), used to
           come back with a stray carriage return on the end of every line - and
           READCSV, which reads through READSEQ, put it on the last field of
           every row, so "B1" compared unequal to "B1".  A carriage return that
           is not followed by a line feed is still returned as data.

           What it does not do: files with ordinary Linux line endings read
           exactly as before, and WRITESEQ still writes LF alone.

           ED's return code for a command error is now negative, as every other
           command's is, so a program or paragraph that tests for a failed ED
           command sees one.


11 Sep 26  SIGNING ON FROM A MODERN TERMINAL.

           Most graphical terminals and ssh clients set TERM to a type such as
           xterm-256color, which SD does not ship a definition for.  Until now
           that left the sign-on sequence with no terminal capabilities at all:
           the screen was not cleared, and backspace at a prompt during sign-on
           erased nothing.  It looked fine once you reached the colon prompt,
           because your account's LOGIN paragraph sets the type again - but that
           runs too late for sign-on itself.  A type SD has no definition for now
           falls back to linux, the same type the LOGIN paragraph uses.

           What it does not do: it does not add definitions for more terminal
           types, and a TERM that SD does know is used exactly as before.


11 Sep 26  F1 SAYS SOMETHING.

           Pressing F1 at an empty command prompt used to do nothing at all,
           which looks like a broken keyboard.  It now says that SD has no
           built-in help system, that LIST VOC shows the commands your account
           has, and where this changelog is.

           What it does not do: it is not a help system, and HELP is still not a
           command; the message names only things that are on the machine.


11 Sep 26  .D AND AN UNFINISHED TRANSACTION.

           .D name, which deletes a saved sentence or paragraph from the VOC,
           now says "not found in VOC" when there is no such record.  It used to
           say nothing and act on whatever record had last been read, so .D with
           a mistyped name could offer to delete a different record.  It also no
           longer leaves a lock behind when the name is not found.

           A BASIC program with a BEGIN TRANSACTION that is never closed now
           fails to compile with "Unterminated transaction construct" instead
           of making the compiler run for ever.

           What it does not do: neither changes a program or paragraph that was
           already correct.


11 Sep 26  DELETE.FILE CAN RUN UNATTENDED.

           DELETE.FILE ... NO.QUERY no longer stops to ask a question.  When
           part of the file is in the system account it now deletes the VOC
           reference, leaves the file itself where it is, and says so (message
           10117) - it no longer assumes you meant yes to a question about other
           accounts' data.  Deleting a file whose name you typed in lower case
           no longer asks "OK to delete DATA portion" and then the same about
           the dictionary: those questions were caused only by the difference in
           case.  When the question is asked, it now shows that Enter means No,
           and it no longer asks again for ever when there is nobody to answer
           it - run from a script it used to print the question millions of
           times.  The same holds for "Use file" in DELETE.FILE, for the three
           "Program is also in ... catalogue. Remove?" questions in CATALOG, and
           for .D's "Delete VOC record?": each shows (y/<n>), and Enter means No.  An index held in a separate directory is now reported if it
           cannot be deleted (message 2636) instead of being left behind
           without a word.  And DELETE.FILE refuses to delete VOC itself however
           you type the name.

           What it does not do: NO.QUERY still does not delete a file whose data
           is somewhere other than its usual place without asking; FORCE is the
           way to say yes to that.


11 Sep 26  TWO MORE FIXES TAKEN FROM SD CORE FOR WINDOWS.

           QSELECT now names the select list it filled.  A successful QSELECT
           ended its message with a dangling "...to select list " and no number,
           although QSELECT takes TO n and the result can be in any list from 0
           to 10, so the one thing you need next was missing.  It now reads
           "14 record(s) selected to select list 3", as SELECT and NSELECT
           already did.

           DELETE.INDEX now accepts an index name typed in any case.  Index
           names are held in upper case, and LIST.INDEX folds case before it
           looks, but DELETE.INDEX did not: "delete.index zzak f1" answered
           "Unrecognised index name (f1)" for an index that LIST.INDEX showed as
           F1.  The two commands now agree.  A name that still matches nothing is
           reported exactly as you typed it.

           What it does not do: neither changes anything else about how the two
           commands select or delete; each was a wrong or missing answer.


10 Sep 26  SMALLER FIXES TAKEN FROM SD CORE FOR WINDOWS.

           CONFIG() given a name longer than eight characters returns an empty
           string with status 1004, as for any unknown name, instead of stopping
           the program.  A file in a directory file whose name holds a % that is
           not one of SD's own escapes is listed under its real name ("draft%1"
           rather than "draft"), and a name ending in % no longer makes SELECT
           read past the end of its buffer.  A WRITE inside a transaction with no
           lock held now says so (message 10151) instead of suggesting the disk
           may be full.  WRITESEQ to a port ends a line with CR and LF, not CR
           alone.  sdtic reports every terminal definition that fails to compile
           and exits with an error, and a bad definition no longer spoils the
           ones after it.  sd -start says so when it cannot start its daemon.

           What it does not do: none of this changes how SD is used or
           configured; each was a fault that either stopped a program or gave a
           wrong answer.

10 Sep 26  NANO JOINS MICRO, AND BOTH FULL-SCREEN EDITORS WORK THE SAME WAY.

           NANO <file> <record> edits a record in nano, as MICRO does in micro,
           and both highlight SD BASIC in a BP file.  They are now one program,
           taken from SD Core for Windows, and it brings three things MICRO did
           not have.  Value, subvalue and text marks show in the editor as ~~,
           ~` and ~! and turn back into marks when the record is saved, so a
           record holding marks is safe to edit.  The working copy in $HOLD is
           removed however the editor is left.  And a missing editor, a session
           with no terminal, or an account without OS-ON is told so before
           anything is written.  EDIT is still the line editor ED.

           What it does not do: nano's highlighting is installed for everyone
           only where /usr/share/nano exists; micro's is copied into each user's
           own configuration the first time they use MICRO.  A STANDARD account
           has neither verb.  A record whose name contains a single quote has to
           be edited with ED.


10 Sep 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT CAN REMOVE THE USER'S HOME DIRECTORY.

           DELETE.ACCOUNT <account> REMOVE.HOME also removes the home directory
           of the account's Linux user.  The one confirmation names the home
           directory, and afterwards SD says whether it really is gone.  Without
           REMOVE.HOME the home directory is left alone, as before.

           What it does not do: it removes a home directory only for a Linux user
           that SD itself created, and only when that home is the ordinary
           /home/<user>.  For any other user REMOVE.HOME is refused before the
           question is asked, and nothing of the user is removed.


10 Sep 26  A ROUND OF CHANGES TO MATCH SD CORE FOR WINDOWS.

           SD Core for Linux follows SD Core for Windows wherever the two do the
           same job.  An audit found places where they had drifted apart, and
           these are the ones a user will notice.

           Administration verbs are for administrators only.  SH, "!", CONFIG,
           LISTU, LIST.READU, LIST.LOCKS, CLEAR.LOCKS, LOCK, SET.DATE and
           CLEAN.ACCOUNT are no longer given to STANDARD or PROGRAMMER accounts;
           an ADMINISTRATOR account gets them.  An administrator who wants one
           person to have one of these can copy that verb into the person's VOC.

           UPDATE.ACCOUNT is now UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, the name SD Core for Windows
           uses.  UPDATE.ACCOUNTS ALL, typed in SDSYS by an administrator, updates
           every registered account without asking first and says how many it
           updated.  Any other word after the verb is refused.

           NLS, SET.LANGUAGE and LOAD.LANGUAGE are gone: SD is English only.

           Every yes/no question now shows which answer pressing Enter gives -
           "(y/<n>)" - and Enter gives it.  Several questions used to ask again
           for ever on an empty answer.  The default is always the answer that
           changes nothing.

           Accounts created from now on keep the descriptions on their VOC
           records ("File - Account register" rather than a bare "F"), and a VOC
           update no longer strips them.

           DELETE.ACCOUNT asks one question instead of up to three, and the
           question names what will be deleted.  It deletes the account's Linux
           user too, but only a user SD itself created: CREATE.ACCOUNT now marks
           the users it makes, and any other user - including the person who
           installed SD - is left in place, with a message saying so.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT takes SH-ON and OS-ON, so a new account can be given
           the shell or OS.EXECUTE when it is made.  MODIFY.ACCOUNT's SH-ON and
           OS-ON now report where both settings stand, and refuse a group
           account, which has no person of its own to grant them to.

           DELETE.CATALOG checks every name before deleting any, so a list that
           mixes your own entries with global ones deletes nothing instead of
           stopping half way.

           Several messages have new numbers, so that a message number means the
           same thing in SD Core for Linux as in SD Core for Windows.

           What it does not do: existing accounts keep the verbs and VOC records
           they already have - an update adds and replaces but never removes, and
           changing an account's tier is what brings its verbs into line.  Linux
           users created before this change carry no mark, so DELETE.ACCOUNT
           leaves them in place; remove one with userdel if it should go.
           DELETE.ACCOUNT does not remove the Linux user's home directory.


10 Sep 26  MICRO WORKS AGAIN, AND ADMINISTRATORS REACH THE SYSTEM WITHOUT SUDO.

           MICRO started the editor through SD's shell command, which is closed
           to anyone who has not been given shell access.  In an ordinary
           session it printed "not permitted to use the operating system shell"
           and then "Record was not saved" without ever opening the editor.  It
           now starts the editor itself, and says plainly when it cannot: the
           session has no terminal, the account may not run programs outside SD
           (MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> OS-ON grants that), or micro is not
           installed.  It also finds the file however its name is typed, so
           "micro bp test" edits BP.

           An administrator working in their own account without sudo now has
           the shell (SH and "!"), OS.EXECUTE and MICRO.  The rules already said
           an administrator's operating-system access comes with the tier and
           cannot be switched off, but in practice it was only there under
           "sudo sd".

           What it does not do: this access belongs to the administrator's own
           account.  After LOGTO into an account that is not an administrator's,
           the shell and MICRO follow that account's own settings.  A STANDARD
           account still has no MICRO verb.  MICRO will not edit a record whose
           name contains a single quote; use ED for that.


10 Sep 26  CHANGING AN ACCOUNT'S TIER NOW CHANGES ITS VERBS AT ONCE.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> STANDARD, PROGRAMMER or ADMINISTRATOR now
           brings the account's verbs into line with the new tier straight away.
           Before, the tier changed but the account kept exactly the verbs it
           had, so a STANDARD account raised to PROGRAMMER still could not
           compile, and a PROGRAMMER lowered to STANDARD still could.  Raising a
           tier copies in the verbs it adds; lowering one removes them.  The
           command reports how many VOC records it added, removed and left
           alone.

           What it does not do: when lowering a tier it removes a verb only if
           the account's record is still exactly as SD wrote it.  A record
           someone has edited or replaced under that name is left in place and
           counted as "left alone" - it is never silently destroyed, but it does
           stay usable.  File pointers and anything else the account has added
           are not touched.  If the account's VOC cannot be opened, the tier is
           not changed.


10 Sep 26  A VOC UPDATE NO LONGER UNDOES AN ACCOUNT'S TIER.

           After an upgrade, logging in to an account whose VOC is at an older
           release asks "Update VOC to new release?", and UPDATE.ACCOUNTS does the
           same thing on request.  Both copied the whole of the shipped VOC into
           the account, so a STANDARD account that answered Y got back every verb
           it had been created without - BASIC, CATALOG, RUN, the editors - and no
           privilege was needed to do it.  The update now applies the account's
           tier exactly as CREATE.ACCOUNT does.

           It also fixes an old fault in the same update: a verb whose record was
           identical to the one before it (CATALOGUE after CATALOG, for example)
           could be skipped, leaving the account without it.

           What it does not do: an update adds and replaces, it never removes, so
           an account already holding verbs its tier would not give it keeps them.
           An account created before tiers existed is updated with the full set.


10 Sep 26  ACCOUNTS NOW GET A VERB SET THAT MATCHES THEIR TIER.

           The verbs a new account receives now depend on its tier.  A STANDARD
           account gets the everyday verbs but not the ones for building or
           administering a system - no BASIC, CATALOG, RUN, the file and index
           commands, the editors, and so on: it can run an application, not build
           one.  A PROGRAMMER account gets the full set, and an ADMINISTRATOR
           account gets that plus the account-management verbs (CREATE.ACCOUNT,
           DELETE.ACCOUNT, MODIFY.ACCOUNT, UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, UNLOCK, and the other
           administration verbs listed in the entry above).

           What it does not do: an account created before this change keeps the
           verbs it was created with.  Changing its tier with MODIFY.ACCOUNT
           brings them into line (see the entries above).


10 Sep 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NOW CREATES THE OPERATING-SYSTEM USER TOO.

           In SD a user account and its operating-system login are the same
           thing, so CREATE.ACCOUNT now creates the OS user as part of creating
           the account.  The OS user no longer has to exist first, and there is
           no longer a setting (CREATUSR) to turn this on - it just happens.  You
           are prompted for the new user's password.

           What it does not do: CREATE.ACCOUNT with NO.QUERY still will not create
           an OS user, because creating one needs the password prompt that
           NO.QUERY suppresses.  For an unattended CREATE.ACCOUNT ... NO.QUERY,
           pre-create the OS user; otherwise leave NO.QUERY off.


10 Sep 26  THE LINUX CLIENT LIBRARY HAS BEEN HARDENED.

           The library that programs use to reach SD over the network
           (sdclilib.so) has been replaced with a hardened version.  It now
           checks the length of what the server sends before trusting it,
           prevents a class of buffer overflows, copes with network reads that
           arrive in pieces or are interrupted, drops a connection cleanly if it
           loses sync instead of carrying on, enforces the server's maximum
           record size, and tells a server-side failure apart from a client-side
           one.  The default connection port is 4243.

           What it does not do: it does not change how you call the library -
           existing programs keep working unchanged.  It is the same library
           with sturdier handling of bad or partial input.


10 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER CAN NOW OPEN SSH AND THE NETWORK API TO OTHER COMPUTERS.

           The installer asks two questions.  "Allow ssh access from other
           computers?" - yes turns the ssh service on at every boot and opens
           port 22 in the firewall.  "Allow API access from other computers?" -
           yes opens the SD network API (the interface SD client programs use)
           on TCP port 4243 to the network.  Both default to no.

           Left at the defaults, ssh is untouched and the API listens only on
           this machine (127.0.0.1 port 4243): programs on the same computer can
           use it, nothing on the network can.  A remote program can still reach
           it by tunnelling over ssh.

           A connection to the API always has to log in with an SD user name and
           password, whether it arrives locally or over the network.

           What it does not do: it does not switch your firewall on.  If ufw is
           not running the "allow" rules are simply recorded for when it is.  It
           exposes nothing by default - you have to answer yes.


10 Sep 26  OVER SSH, NON-ADMINISTRATOR ACCOUNTS ARE NOW HELD INSIDE SD.

           SD can decide that a STANDARD or PROGRAMMER account may not reach the
           operating-system shell.  Until now that decision stopped at the door
           of SD: the same account could connect over ssh and land at an
           ordinary shell prompt, never entering SD, so the restriction did not
           hold at the edge of the machine.  The installer now adds a block to
           the ssh configuration that sends every non-administrator SD account
           straight into SD on an ssh login.  An administrator keeps a normal
           shell.  Someone SD has granted shell access (MODIFY.ACCOUNT SH-ON)
           still reaches one, through SD, as themselves.

           What it does not do: it does not change who may connect or log in -
           only what a non-administrator gets once connected.  It leaves local
           console logins alone.  Because the ssh session is handed straight to
           SD, file-copy tools that run over ssh (scp, sftp) no longer work for
           non-administrator accounts; that follows from the decision rather
           than being a separate setting.  If you have already customised your
           ssh configuration, the installer will NOT edit it - it tells you so
           and leaves it to you, rather than changing a file you are managing.
           Your original ssh configuration is kept as
           /etc/ssh/sshd_config.before-sd, and uninstalling removes the block.


10 Sep 26  A NEW INSTALL REGISTERS THE INSTALLING USER AS AN SD ADMINISTRATOR.

           Installing SD used to leave the person who ran the installer with an
           ordinary STANDARD account and no administrator registered at all.
           SD coped by granting administrator rights for the first session and
           saying that no administrator was registered.  That fallback still
           exists, but it is now a fallback: an install registers the
           installing user as an ADMINISTRATOR, which is both the account tier
           and membership of the sdadmin group.  An upgrade that keeps its
           existing accounts does not change any of them, so reinstalling does
           not promote anybody - and the sdadmin group survives that upgrade,
           so existing administrators do not have to be promoted again.

           The installer also now says plainly, at the start, if the person
           running it cannot use sudo, instead of failing part-way through with
           a system error.

           A fresh install no longer stops part-way through when /home/sd
           already exists - which it does when an uninstall kept your SD
           configuration.  The installer now makes the account directories
           whatever state /home/sd is in, instead of only when /home/sd is
           missing.

09 Sep 26  OPERATING-SYSTEM ACCESS IS NOW A PER-ACCOUNT PRIVILEGE.

           OS.EXECUTE - the SD BASIC statement that runs an operating-system
           command - and the SH verb and "!" at the command prompt are now off
           for everyone except administrators and SD's own system programs.
           Before this, any program could run OS.EXECUTE, so the boundary the
           account tiers are meant to draw did not hold: a standard or programmer
           account could reach a shell from a program it wrote, whatever its tier
           allowed inside SD.  A program that is neither an administrator's nor
           part of SD is now refused with "not permitted to use OS.EXECUTE", and
           a non-administrator using SH at the prompt is told they are "not
           permitted to use the operating system shell".

           An administrator can give either privilege to a named account:

               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> OS-ON      allow OS.EXECUTE
               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> OS-OFF     take it away
               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SH-ON      allow SH and "!"
               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SH-OFF     take it away

           The two are independent, so SH-OFF says nothing about OS.EXECUTE.
           Administrators always have both, so the four forms refuse an
           administrator account and say so.  A grant takes effect the next time
           the account is entered, not for a session already running in it.

09 Sep 26  SD BASIC IS SYNTAX HIGHLIGHTED IN THE EDITOR.

           MICRO now colours SD BASIC: keywords, strings, numbers and comments.
           The colours come from a file generated out of the compiler's own
           tables, so what is highlighted is what the compiler actually
           accepts, rather than a list somebody typed and then let drift.

           It only applies to records edited from a BP file - a program - which
           is how SD knows the record is BASIC.  A VOC or data record gets no
           colouring, which is the honest answer for something that is not a
           program.

           You do not have to do anything.  The first time you edit a program
           with MICRO, SD copies the colour file into your own micro
           configuration.  It is per-user because that is the only place micro
           reads them from, and it is copied rather than forced, so if you
           later edit that file yourself your version is kept until SD's own
           genuinely changes.

09 Sep 26  ACCOUNT MAINTENANCE NO LONGER CALLS sudo DIRECTLY, AND TWO THINGS
           THAT ONLY WORKED ON DEBIAN NOW WORK EVERYWHERE.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT, MODIFY.ACCOUNT and DELETE.ACCOUNT used to run eight
           different privileged commands through sudo - useradd, userdel,
           passwd, groupadd, groupdel, usermod, deluser and chmod.  None of
           those is restricted by its arguments, so a system that let SD run
           them let SD run them on anything: "sudo passwd root" is the whole
           machine.

           They now go through one command that SD owns and that checks what it
           is being asked to do.  It refuses root, SD's own sdsys account and
           every system account as a target; it will only touch groups that
           belong to SD, and never SD's own sdusers and sdadmin groups; and it
           resolves a path before setting the group bit on it, so a symlink
           cannot point it somewhere else.  If you administer SD you will see
           it name the command it resolved before it runs.

           TWO REAL BUGS GO WITH IT.  Removing a user from a group used the
           deluser command, which exists on Debian and Ubuntu and not on Arch,
           Fedora or RHEL - so MODIFY.ACCOUNT's DELETE, and demoting an
           administrator, simply failed there.  And creating an account group
           used "groupadd -U", which needs a recent shadow-utils.  Both now use
           gpasswd, which is present everywhere.

09 Sep 26  SETTING A PASSWORD AND CREATING AN OPERATING SYSTEM USER NOW REQUIRE
           ADMINISTRATOR RIGHTS IN THEIR OWN RIGHT.

           Two of SD's internal functions - the ones behind CREATE.ACCOUNT that
           add a Linux user and set its password - are in the global catalogue,
           which means any account can call them from a BASIC program.  Neither
           checked who was calling.  The only thing protecting them was that
           CREATE.ACCOUNT, the verb, checks - so the check was on the door and
           not on the room.

           A program written in an ordinary account could therefore make SD run
           "sudo passwd someone" or "sudo useradd".  On a machine where the
           caller can use sudo that succeeded; where they cannot, sudo stopped
           and asked for a password inside an SD session, which is not a place
           that can answer one.

           Both now refuse unless the caller is an SD administrator, and they
           report it the way they report every other failure - by returning
           false and setting STATUS().

09 Sep 26  THIS IS SD CORE, VERSION L1.0-0.

           The sign-on line now reads

               SD Core, the Essential Multivalue String Database, version L1.0-0

           and "sd --version" answers L1.0-0 to match.  It used to say 1.0-2,
           which is not this project's number at all - it is the release of the
           SD this work was built from.  The binary was telling you the version
           of something else.

           THE L IS DELIBERATE AND IT IS NOT GOING TO CATCH UP.  Our numbering
           is a separate sequence from upstream's, which is what the letter
           marks - exactly as SD Core for Windows uses W.  So upstream reaching
           1.0-3, or any later number, is not a version this system is behind.

           The "(AI modified)" that used to follow the version is gone.  It
           distinguished this from stock SD; the L does that now.

           If you have accounts created before this update, the first time you
           log in to one SD will tell you the account is at release level 1.0-2
           and ask whether to update it.  Answer Y.  That is the normal way an
           existing account takes on new VOC entries, and it is not a sign that
           anything is wrong.

09 Sep 26  sd -internal NOW REQUIRES root, AND SO DOES EVERY OTHER PRIVILEGED
           sd OPTION ON EVERY SYSTEM.

           -internal puts SD's compiler into the mode that accepts $INTERNAL
           programs, which are the ones allowed to call KERNEL.  It had no
           privilege check, so any user could compile a short program that
           granted itself SD administrator rights - which made the check
           described in the entry below, and every other check of those
           rights, something a user could simply step around.  It now asks the
           same question -i has always asked.

           If you compile SD's own internals - the programs in GPL.BP - you
           now need sudo to do it.  Installing SD is unaffected: the installer
           already ran these steps as root.  Nothing about compiling your own
           programs changes; ordinary BASIC never used this option.

           If you build SD from source and want that check out of your way
           while you work, build with

               make EXTRA_C_FLAGS=-DSD_DEV_BUILD

           A binary built that way says so - "sd --version" reports DEVELOPER
           BUILD and every use of -internal prints a line saying the check was
           skipped.  Do not install one, and if you are reporting a bug please
           include the "sd --version" output so we can see which you are on.
           A plain "make" is unaffected, and the installer always builds from
           a fresh checkout, so what you install is never a developer build.

           IN THE SAME CHANGE, "administrator" for these options means root and
           only root.  SD used to also accept membership of a group called
           admin.  SD does not create or manage a group of that name, and on
           some Linux distributions admin is an old name for the sudo group -
           so on those systems the option checks above were weaker than on
           others, with nothing to tell you which kind of system you were on.
           SD's own group, sdadmin, is deliberately not used here: these are
           operating system operations - start, stop, kill a user, restart,
           bootstrap install - rather than SD ones.

09 Sep 26  WHO COUNTS AS AN ADMINISTRATOR HAS CHANGED.  STARTING SD WITH sudo
           IS NO LONGER ENOUGH ON ITS OWN.

           Until now, any session that reached SD as root was an administrator.
           That is no longer the test.  An administrator is a person who is
           BOTH a member of the sdadmin group - the group SD's own sudoers rule
           names - AND recorded in SD's account register as an ADMINISTRATOR.
           Root is still necessary; it is no longer sufficient.  This is how SD
           Core for Windows has always worked: you can hold the operating
           system's administrator rights and still have no rights inside SD.

           You register an account as an administrator either when you create
           it or afterwards:

               CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> ADMINISTRATOR
               MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> ADMINISTRATOR

           Either records the tier in the register and adds that person to the
           sdadmin group.  MODIFY.ACCOUNT also takes PROGRAMMER and STANDARD,
           and an account can move between the three in one command with no
           intermediate step.  Going down from ADMINISTRATOR removes the person
           from sdadmin again.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT is the one that matters on a system you already have,
           because CREATE.ACCOUNT refuses a name that is already in the account
           register - it will tell you the account exists.  You do not have to
           delete an account to change what it is.

           THE FIRST ADMINISTRATOR IS HANDLED FOR YOU, SO NOTHING TO DO BEFORE
           UPGRADING.  On a system where no account is registered as an
           administrator yet - which is every system installed before this
           update - starting SD with sudo still grants the rights, and SD
           prints a line saying that it has done so and how to register a real
           administrator.  That arm switches itself off the moment the first
           ADMINISTRATOR account exists.  You cannot be locked out by it.

           If your session is refused administrator rights, SD says so as it
           starts rather than leaving you to find out when a command fails -
           and it says WHICH of the two requirements you did not meet, because
           being told "you are not a registered administrator" when you are
           registered and merely missing from the group sends you to look in
           the wrong place.  There are three things it can tell you: you are
           not registered as an administrator; you are registered but are not
           in the sdadmin group; or the account register could not be read at
           all, which is not the same as it saying no.

           Whichever it is, it appears BELOW the sign-on banner, between two
           rule lines and with a blank line either side.  It used to print
           above the banner, where it read as one more line of the boilerplate
           everybody skips.

           Three checks now read the new test instead of asking whether the
           process is root, which was never the same question: CATALOG and
           DELETE.CATALOG on the global catalogue, and LOGTO SDSYS.

           WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO.  It does not change what is in an account's
           VOC - every account still gets the same verbs whatever its tier -
           and it does not yet refuse a session to somebody who is not a
           registered SD user.  Accounts created before this update carry no
           tier, which is exactly the case the first-administrator arm above
           covers.

           SUSPENDED is not here yet.  SD Core for Windows has a fourth level
           that denies access while leaving the account intact, and this
           version does not: it would need the doors it is meant to close, and
           on Linux one of those is ssh, which is still being worked out.  A
           SUSPENDED that recorded a level and shut no door would be worse than
           not having it.

09 Sep 26  PROC, SED, UPDATE.RECORD AND MODIFY HAVE BEEN REMOVED.

           SED was the full-screen editor, and UPDATE.RECORD and MODIFY were
           full-screen record editors - MODIFY the one inherited from OpenQM.
           All three are gone, along with the PROC language.  LISTPQ, the
           sentence that listed the PROCs in a VOC, goes with PROC.

           A VOC item of type PQ now says that PROC is not supported instead of
           running.  Your PQ records are left alone: it is the interpreter that
           has gone, not the records, and SD says so by name rather than
           complaining that the record is invalid.

           ED, the line editor, is not affected and is now the way to edit a
           record.

           Four names are close enough to invite confusion and NONE of them is
           affected:

           * MODIFY.ACCOUNT and MODIFY.PASSWORD are different verbs with
             different programs behind them, and both remain.
           * LIST, COUNT, SELECT and SORT are the query processor, which is a
             different thing from PROC despite the similar name.
           * PROCREAD and PROCWRITE, the two BASIC statements, still compile.
             With the interpreter gone PROCREAD simply reports that it is not
             running inside a PROC, which is what it already did anywhere else.
             Nothing you have compiled stops loading.
           * The PHANTOM debugger is untouched.

           An account created before this update keeps whatever was in its VOC
           at the time - SD only ever adds records at an update, never removes
           them.

09 Sep 26  SD NO LONGER CLAIMS TO SUPPORT VIRTUAL FILE SYSTEMS.

           SD advertised a "virtual file system": you could write a VOC file
           pointer whose pathname began VFS:, and LISTF would report its type as
           VFS.  Nothing behind that was ever built.  No part of SD has ever
           looked at a pathname to see whether it begins with VFS:, no handler
           was ever loaded, and the three error codes reserved for VFS problems
           could not be raised by anything.  So the first thing you saw said the
           pointer was a valid virtual file system, and then OPEN refused it
           with an unrelated path error.

           The claim is now gone rather than the feature, because there was no
           feature.  FTYPE no longer returns VFS, the FL$TYPE.VFS file type is
           withdrawn, and a VFS: pathname is turned into an absolute path like
           any other - which is what SD always actually required.  The template
           class module for writing a VFS handler is no longer shipped.

           What this does not do: nothing that worked stops working.  A VFS:
           pointer failed to open before this change and still fails, only now
           it is described honestly from the start.  The three error numbers
           (3038, 3039, 3040) are retired rather than handed to something else,
           so a program that compares a status against one of those numbers
           still never matches - which is what it already did.  The names
           ER$VFS.NAME, ER$VFS.CLASS, ER$VFS.NGLBL and FL$TYPE.VFS are gone from
           the include files, so a program that spells one out will no longer
           compile cleanly; nothing shipped with SD used them.

09 Sep 26  SDNET - REMOTE FILE ACCESS OVER THE NETWORK - HAS BEEN REMOVED.

           SD could open a file held on another SD server by naming it
           server;file in a VOC pointer, over a private protocol on port 4245.
           That whole facility is gone, along with the verbs that configured it:
           SET.SERVER, DELETE.SERVER and LIST.SERVERS.  A VOC pointer written in
           the server;file form now fails to open like any other bad path.

           Why: it was an unmaintained network path, its per-server passwords
           were stored in sd.conf with a simple letter substitution that is not
           encryption, and it could not be turned off - the NETFILES setting was
           read at startup and then never consulted.  Removing it serves the
           smaller-system goal and closes a weak spot at the same time.

           The NETFILES line in sd.conf is still accepted and ignored, so an
           existing configuration file still loads.  If you used SDNet, remote
           access is no longer available; copy the data across instead.

09 Sep 26  THE TAPE AND RESTORE SUBSYSTEM HAS BEEN REMOVED.

           SD no longer ships the tape verbs - ACCOUNT.SAVE, ACCOUNT.RESTORE,
           FILE.SAVE, RESTORE.ACCOUNTS, SET.DEVICE, the T.* family (T.ATT,
           T.REW, T.DUMP and the rest) - or the programs behind them.  They were
           an optional part of the install: you were asked "Install TAPE and
           RESTORE subsystem?" and most installs said no.  The question is gone
           and so are the programs.

           This is part of making SD a smaller system.  Tape was a way of moving
           whole accounts to and from magnetic tape devices, which is not how
           anyone backs up or transfers data now; ordinary file copies and the
           account directory serve that purpose.  If you had installed it and
           relied on a T.* verb, it is no longer present.

09 Sep 26  ENCRYPTION, SDEXT AND EMBEDDED-PYTHON ERRORS NOW SHOW THEIR MESSAGE
           TEXT INSTEAD OF A BARE NUMBER.

           The error codes added for the encryption, SDEXT and embedded-Python
           features had no message text, so an error from any of them printed
           just a number - and the number carried the wrong sign, positive where
           every other SD error code is negative.  A program catching one by
           value would not have matched it.

           Both are fixed.  Those codes now read back the way the rest do: the
           right (negative) number and a sentence of text.  Nothing that already
           worked changes - these are the codes that showed nothing useful
           before.

           Behind this: the BASIC copies of the error, revision and opcode
           tables (SYSCOM/ERR.H, GPL.BP/ERRTEXT.H, GPL.BP/REVSTAMP.H,
           GPL.BP/OPCODES.H) are generated from the C sources rather than kept in
           step by hand, which is how they had drifted.  A build now checks they
           match and stops if they do not, so this cannot silently come back.

09 Sep 26  A TRANSACTION THAT FAILS PART WAY NOW PUTS BACK WHAT IT HAD
           ALREADY WRITTEN.

           A transaction is meant to be all or nothing.  Until now, if a COMMIT
           got part way through and then could not apply one of its actions - a
           record locked by something else, a read-only file, a disk problem -
           it stopped there and left everything it had already applied on disk.
           The records before the failure were written; the ones after it were
           not.  There was no way to undo it and nothing told you which was
           which.

           SD now records what each record held immediately before the commit
           overwrote it, and puts them back if the commit fails.  A record that
           existed is restored to its previous contents.  A record the
           transaction was creating is removed again, since it never existed.

           WHAT IS WRITTEN TO THE ERROR LOG.  Whether or not everything could be
           put back, SD writes a line to sdsys/errlog saying so:

               Transaction commit failed part way: 3 record(s) restored,
               1 removed, 0 could not be undone

           If a particular record could not be put back - because whatever
           stopped the commit is also stopping the repair - it is named
           individually, with its file, so you know exactly what to look at.  SD
           undoes everything else it can rather than stopping at the first
           problem.

           One thing worth knowing: the record the commit actually failed on is
           also restored, and so may appear in that count.  A write that fails
           half way can leave a record in a state nothing can describe, so SD
           puts it back rather than assuming it was untouched.

           AND A FAILED COMMIT NO LONGER HOLDS ITS RECORD LOCKS.  The locks a
           transaction took used to stay held for the life of the session after
           a commit failed, so the records nobody could finish committing were
           also records nobody could reach to repair.  They are now released as
           part of the same rollback.

08 Sep 26  A COMMIT INSIDE ANOTHER TRANSACTION NO LONGER THROWS AWAY THE
           OUTER TRANSACTION'S WRITES.

           If you began a transaction, wrote a record, began a second
           transaction inside it, and committed that inner one, the outer
           transaction was quietly abandoned at that point.  Its writes were
           then lost when you committed it - no error, no warning, nothing in
           the log.  The inner records landed and the outer ones did not.

           That is the one thing a transaction exists to prevent, so if you
           have avoided nesting transactions because results looked
           inconsistent, this is why.

           A commit now ends only its own transaction and puts you back in the
           one that contained it, which is what a rollback has always done.

           system(1008), the transaction nesting level, is also correct again.
           It only ever counted upwards before - raised when a transaction
           began and never lowered when one was committed - so after your first
           commit it could not be used to ask whether you were inside a
           transaction.  system(1007) was unaffected and stays the reliable
           test on older releases.

08 Sep 26  RECORDS WRITTEN OR DELETED INSIDE A TRANSACTION, IN A DIRECTORY
           FILE, WITH CERTAIN CHARACTERS IN THE RECORD ID.

           This affects directory files only, and only record ids containing
           one of these characters:

               *  ,  =  >  <  %  /  +  :  ;  ?  \  "

           or an id starting with a dot or a tilde.  In a directory file each
           record is a separate file on disk, and because those characters are
           awkward or illegal in a filename, SD stores them encoded - the id "="
           is held in a file called "%E", and SD converts back and forth for you
           so you never see it.

           That conversion was not being done for a write or a delete made
           inside a transaction.

           A WRITE inside a transaction stored the record under the raw id
           instead of the encoded one.  The write reported success, but a later
           read of the same id looked in the encoded place, found nothing, and
           reported the record as missing.

           A DELETE inside a transaction tried to remove the raw name, which was
           not there.  SD treats "already gone" as success, so the delete
           reported success and the record was still there afterwards.

           Both are fixed.  Neither needed anything to go wrong to happen - the
           transactions committed normally - and neither produced an error, a
           status or a log entry, so a site that hit this would have seen only a
           record that would not read back, or one that would not stay deleted.

           The same statements OUTSIDE a transaction were always correct, and
           records already on disk are unaffected.

08 Sep 26  A DELETE THAT FAILS INSIDE A TRANSACTION IS NO LONGER REPORTED AS
           DONE.

           Deleting a record from a directory file inside a transaction did not
           check whether the delete actually happened.  If the file was
           read-only, protected, or otherwise could not be removed, the commit
           reported success and the record stayed on disk - so a later read,
           select or list returned a record you had been told was gone.

           It now reports the error, the same way a delete outside a transaction
           always has.

08 Sep 26  A FAILURE WHILE EXTENDING AN INDEX NO LONGER DAMAGES THE INDEX.

           When SD needed a new block for an alternate key index and could not
           get one - a disk error, a full disk, a file held open by something
           else - it carried on and used block zero.  Block zero is the index
           header.  The write went over it, and the index was then permanently
           damaged: every select, list or query on that key read from a header
           that had a data block written on top of it.

           The error was always reported, but only after the damage was done,
           so the message named a temporary problem and left a permanent one.

           SD now stops the operation and reports the error before writing
           anything.  The index is left as it was.

           THERE WAS A SECOND WAY IN, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS IS MORE THAN A
           MISSING TEST.  One of the three ways the allocator can fail handed
           back a real node number rather than zero - the head of the free
           chain, which it had not removed from that chain.  Checking the
           answer at each of the seven places that ask for a node could not
           have caught that, because the answer looked valid.  The allocator
           now reports failure the same way on all three paths.

           A healthy disk never reached any of this, and nothing about normal
           indexing changes.  If you have an index that has been behaving oddly
           after a disk problem, rebuild it.

08 Sep 26  WEOFSEQ AND OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE NO LONGER REPORT SUCCESS WHEN THEY
           FAILED TO SHORTEN THE FILE.

           Both of these do their work by truncating a file, and neither looked
           at whether the truncate had worked.

           WEOFSEQ put 0 - success - on the stack whatever happened, so a
           program had no way to find out.  What this costs is a stale tail:
           the old content past the new end survives, so a file written by a
           later and shorter run can still carry rows from an earlier, longer
           one.  A file produced that way is the answer somebody takes away,
           which is why this was worth fixing even though nobody has reported
           it.

           OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE promises the previous content is gone.  If the
           truncate failed the old file stayed as it was, and later writes
           replaced only the front of it.

           Both now report the failure the way the rest of these statements
           already do: STATUS() gives the I/O error, an ON ERROR clause runs,
           and a statement written without one stops rather than carrying on.
           Nothing that used to work behaves differently - the change is only
           visible when the truncate actually fails, which needs a read-only
           file, a mandatory lock or a full disk.

08 Sep 26  ERROR MESSAGES ARE NO LONGER CUT OFF AFTER THE FIRST LINE.

           Anything SD reported as a program error was truncated at about 84
           characters, so a message written as three lines lost the last two -
           usually the part saying what to do about it.

           The room was always there: the buffer holds 241 characters and the
           limit had been set to 84 by a typing slip, a + where a * was meant.
           Long messages now print in full.

08 Sep 26  "sd -stop" NO LONGER RISKS KILLING THE PROGRAM THAT RAN IT.

           Stopping SD signals every session in its user table.  It did that
           without checking that the recorded process id was real, and a stale
           entry can carry a process id of 0 - which on Linux does not mean
           "nobody", it means EVERY PROCESS IN MY PROCESS GROUP.

           So "sd -stop" run from a script or a terminal could terminate the
           script, the shell, or anything else running alongside it, and say
           nothing about having done so.  SD now signals only a real process id.

           WHETHER YOU EVER HIT THIS depends on whether a session ever died in a
           way that left its entry behind with no process id.  Nothing about a
           normal stop changes.

08 Sep 26  "sd -cleanup" NOW RELEASES A DEAD SESSION'S TASK LOCKS.

           It already gave back the file, record and group locks a lost session
           was holding, but never its task locks - so a LOCK taken by a program
           that was then killed stayed taken, and no recovery an administrator
           had would shift it.  A nightly job guarded by LOCK 3 would not run
           again until SD was restarted.

           IF YOU HAVE BEEN RESTARTING SD TO CLEAR A STUCK TASK LOCK, you should
           not need to any more.  It was comparing the lock against the wrong
           session's number - the one running the cleanup rather than the one
           being cleaned up.

08 Sep 26  GLOBAL CATALOGUING NOW REQUIRES ADMINISTRATOR RIGHTS WHICHEVER WAY
           YOU ASK FOR IT, AND DELETE.CATALOG REQUIRES THEM AT ALL.

           CATALOG already required them for the spelled-out form,
           "CATALOG BP MYPROG GLOBAL".  It did NOT require them when the same
           thing was asked for by putting a *, !, _ or $ in front of the name -
           "CATALOG BP $MYPROG" - which is the form most people use.

           DELETE.CATALOG required nothing at all, by either route.

           THIS MATTERS BECAUSE THE GLOBAL CATALOGUE HOLDS THE PROGRAMS SD RUNS
           FOR EVERYBODY, $LOGIN among them.  Replacing one ran your code in
           every session on the machine, administrators included; deleting one
           stopped everybody signing in.

           NOTHING CHANGES FOR LOCAL AND PRIVATE CATALOGUING, which is what
           programmers use day to day:

               CATALOG BP MYPROG          private catalogue, this account
               CATALOG BP MYPROG LOCAL    this account's VOC

           Both still work, in any account you are allowed to enter.  The only
           thing you can no longer do as an ordinary user is catalogue or delete
           a program whose name starts with *, !, _ or $, because those
           characters mean "system-wide".  Name it without one.

08 Sep 26  "term default" NOW RESTORES 120 x 36, NOT 20 x 24.

           It was setting the page to the smallest size SD allows rather than
           the default, which wrapped every standard report.  It now sets the
           documented width and depth - the same size a session starts with when
           nothing else tells it otherwise.

08 Sep 26  SET.SOCKET.MODE CAN NOW TURN KEEP-ALIVE OFF.

           SET.SOCKET.MODE(skt, SKT$INFO.KEEP.ALIVE, 0) returned 1, for success,
           and switched keep-alive ON.  The value you passed was read and then
           discarded.  SOCKET.INFO(skt, SKT$INFO.KEEP.ALIVE) reads the socket
           itself and had been telling the truth all along, so the two disagreed.

           The setting is now whatever you asked for.  If you have code that
           worked around this by not calling it, the workaround is no longer
           needed.

08 Sep 26  ENCRYPT.FIELD HAS BEEN REMOVED.  IT COULD NEVER WORK.

           The verb was in the account template and pointed at a program called
           $CRYPTO which is not part of this distribution, so typing it answered

               Unable to load '$CRYPTO' object code at line 1550 of $CPROC

           which names neither the verb you typed nor anything you could act on.
           "Verb not found" is the honest answer, and that is what you get now.

           Nothing in the documentation referred to the verb and field encryption
           was not available through it before.  THE ENCRYPT KEYWORD IS A
           DIFFERENT THING and is untouched.


